/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccnp-encor.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ccnp-encor", [["en1",0,1,"A mid-size campus has a single building with 12 access switches and no plans to grow. The team wants to cut cost and hop count while keeping redundancy. Which design fits best?",["A two-tier collapsed core with one redundant switch pair","A three-tier design with separate access, distribution and core layers","A spine-leaf fabric with every access switch acting as a spine","A flat Layer 2 network with one large VLAN spanning every switch"],0,"A collapsed core suits a small, single-building campus: it keeps redundancy while removing a layer. A separate core pays off when many distribution blocks must be interconnected.","Exam topics section 1.0 (enterprise network design)",[null,"A three-tier design adds a separate core layer, which only pays off when many distribution blocks must be interconnected, so it adds cost and hops here.","Spine-leaf is a data center fabric design, and access switches are leaves, not spines; it is overkill for one small campus building.","A flat network with one VLAN everywhere creates a huge broadcast and spanning tree failure domain and gives up the Layer 3 boundary."],2],["en2",0,1,"In a data center spine-leaf (Clos) fabric, how are the switches connected?",["Every spine connects to every other spine, and each leaf connects to one spine","Leaves connect in a ring, and spines connect only to the WAN routers","Every leaf links to every spine, and leaves never link to each other","Each leaf connects to two other leaves, and one spine connects to the core"],2,"In a Clos fabric each leaf uplinks to every spine, so any two leaves are always the same number of hops apart. Spines do not interconnect with each other.","Exam topics section 1.0 (enterprise network design)",["Spines do not connect to each other in a Clos fabric, and each leaf connects to every spine, not just one.","A ring of leaves would make hop counts vary between leaf pairs; spines are the interconnect for all leaves, not WAN-only devices.",null,"Leaves do not link to each other in a spine-leaf fabric, and every spine (not one) connects to every leaf."],1],["en3",0,1,"A pair of Catalyst 9500 switches runs Stateful Switchover (SSO) with dual supervisors. What does SSO provide when the active supervisor fails?",["Both supervisors forward traffic at once, doubling the switch throughput","The standby supervisor takes over with synced state, so forwarding continues","The switch reboots faster because the image is cached in flash","The neighboring routers take over the gateway role for the VLANs through HSRP"],1,"SSO keeps the standby supervisor synchronized so it can take over without resetting line cards. It is not active-active forwarding, and it is separate from FHRP gateway redundancy.","Exam topics section 1.0 (high availability)",["SSO is active/standby: only the active supervisor runs the control plane, so throughput is not doubled.",null,"SSO avoids a reboot altogether by switching to an already synchronized standby; it is not about faster image loading.","HSRP is a first hop redundancy protocol between separate devices and is unrelated to supervisor redundancy inside one chassis."],1],["en4",0,1,"Which statement correctly contrasts the SD-WAN control plane with its data plane?",["The WAN Edge routers exchange routes over OMP, while the Controllers forward user traffic between sites","The Manager forwards user traffic, while the Validator pushes templates to every edge","The Controllers forward traffic over IPsec, while the Manager exchanges routes over BGP","Controllers exchange routes and policy over OMP; WAN Edges carry user traffic in IPsec tunnels"],3,"In Cisco Catalyst SD-WAN, the Controllers (formerly vSmart) run OMP to distribute routes and policy; the WAN Edges build IPsec tunnels and carry the user traffic. The Manager handles management and the Validator handles orchestration and onboarding.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-WAN)",["This swaps the roles: WAN Edges peer with the Controllers over OMP but do not form the control plane themselves, and Controllers never forward user traffic.","The Manager is the management plane and forwards no user traffic, and templates are pushed by the Manager, not the Validator.","Controllers do not forward user traffic, and the SD-WAN overlay routing protocol is OMP, not BGP run by the Manager.",null],2],["en5",0,1,"A new SD-WAN edge router powers up at a branch. Which component first authenticates it and tells it where to find the Controllers and Manager?",["The SD-WAN Manager (formerly vManage)","The SD-WAN Controller (formerly vSmart)","The Catalyst Center assurance engine","The SD-WAN Validator (formerly vBond)"],3,"The Validator is the orchestration plane: it authenticates devices joining the fabric and points them to the Controllers and Manager. The Manager is the management GUI, and the Controllers handle routing and policy.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-WAN)",["The Manager is the management plane for templates and monitoring; a new edge learns how to reach it from the Validator first.","The Controllers handle OMP routing and policy, but a new edge must be authenticated and pointed to them by the Validator before it can join them.","Catalyst Center is the campus controller and has no role in onboarding SD-WAN edge routers.",null],1],["en6",0,1,"In Cisco SD-Access, which protocol forms the fabric control plane that tracks where each endpoint is located?",["VXLAN, with the border node flooding endpoint locations to every edge","LISP, with the control plane node as map server and map resolver","OSPF, with each edge node advertising every host route as an external LSA","CTS, with scalable group tags carrying each endpoint's location"],1,"SD-Access uses LISP for the control plane, VXLAN for the data plane and Cisco TrustSec (SGTs) for policy. The control plane node is the LISP map server and resolver.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-Access)",["VXLAN is the SD-Access data plane encapsulation, and the fabric does not flood endpoint locations to every edge.",null,"OSPF or IS-IS may run in the underlay, but SD-Access does not track endpoints by advertising host routes as external LSAs.","Cisco TrustSec SGTs form the policy plane; they carry group identity, not endpoint location."],1],["en7",0,1,"What does VXLAN provide in an SD-Access fabric?",["The data plane encapsulation that carries traffic across the routed underlay","The control plane database that maps endpoint identifiers to locators","The policy plane that tags traffic with scalable group identifiers at the edge","The management plane that pushes configuration from Catalyst Center"],0,"VXLAN is the SD-Access data plane: it encapsulates frames in UDP across the underlay. LISP is the control plane and TrustSec is the policy plane.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-Access)",[null,"Mapping endpoint identifiers to locators is the job of LISP, the SD-Access control plane.","Tagging traffic with scalable group identifiers is the TrustSec policy plane, although the SGT does ride inside the VXLAN header.","Catalyst Center provisions the fabric through its own management plane; VXLAN is a packet encapsulation, not a configuration mechanism."],1],["en8",0,1,"Compared with a traditional campus, which is a key advantage of SD-Access?",["It removes the need for any underlay routing protocol between the switches","It lets every access switch keep its own manually written ACL set","Policy follows users by group identity, not VLAN or IP, and is pushed centrally","It extends one Layer 2 VLAN across the whole campus using spanning tree between buildings"],2,"SD-Access applies group-based policy (SGTs) centrally through Catalyst Center, independent of IP addressing. It still needs a routed underlay and avoids campus-wide spanning tree.","Exam topics section 1.0 (SD-Access)",["SD-Access still requires a routed underlay (typically IS-IS built by LAN automation) beneath the overlay.","Per-switch hand-written ACLs are the traditional model that SD-Access replaces with centrally defined group policy.",null,"SD-Access avoids campus-wide Layer 2 and spanning tree by using a routed underlay with a VXLAN overlay."],2],["en9",0,1,"Voice packets arrive at a WAN router already marked DSCP EF. The WAN link is congested. Which QoS tool gives the voice traffic strict priority when it is sent out the interface?",["Weighted random early detection on the voice class","Low latency queuing (LLQ) with a priority queue for EF traffic","Class-based policing that drops excess voice packets","Traffic shaping on the ingress interface of the router"],1,"LLQ adds a strict-priority queue to CBWFQ so delay-sensitive EF traffic is sent first. WRED drops packets early to avoid TCP synchronization and is not meant for voice.","Exam topics section 1.0 (QoS)",["WRED drops packets early to manage TCP congestion; dropping voice packets would harm call quality rather than prioritize it.",null,"Policing only limits or drops excess traffic; it does not give the voice queue priority access to the link.","Shaping works on egress traffic and delays packets, and delay is exactly what voice cannot tolerate."],2],["en10",0,1,"A service provider sells a 50 Mb/s service on a 1 Gb/s handoff and drops anything above the contract. What should the customer configure on its egress interface to avoid those drops?",["Policing to 50 Mb/s, which drops or remarks bursts immediately","Marking all traffic as DSCP CS1 so the provider treats it as scavenger","WRED on the provider's ingress interface to smooth the traffic","Shaping to 50 Mb/s, which buffers bursts and sends them later"],3,"Shaping queues excess traffic and releases it at the contracted rate, so the provider's policer does not drop it. Policing on the customer side would still drop the bursts.","Exam topics section 1.0 (QoS)",["Policing on the customer side still drops the bursts, just earlier; it does not smooth traffic to the contracted rate.","Remarking everything as scavenger would lower the priority of all traffic and does nothing to keep it under 50 Mb/s.","The customer cannot configure the provider's interface, and WRED manages queue depth, not conformance to a rate.",null],3],["en11",0,1,"Which table does Cisco Express Forwarding use to rewrite the Layer 2 header of a packet it is about to forward?",["The RIB, which holds every route learned from the routing protocols","The CAM table, which maps VLAN and MAC address pairs to ports","The adjacency table, which holds next-hop Layer 2 rewrite data","The TCAM, which holds the ACL and QoS match entries"],2,"CEF combines the FIB (derived from the RIB) for the next-hop lookup with the adjacency table for the Layer 2 rewrite. The CAM table is for Layer 2 switching.","Exam topics section 1.0 (hardware and software switching)",["The RIB holds routes from the routing protocols; CEF builds the FIB from it but the RIB carries no Layer 2 rewrite data.","The CAM table is for Layer 2 MAC-to-port switching decisions, not for building the rewrite header of a routed packet.",null,"TCAM holds ACL, QoS and prefix match entries for lookups, not the next-hop MAC rewrite information."],1],["en12",0,1,"Why do Catalyst switches store ACL and QoS entries in TCAM rather than in a standard CAM table?",["TCAM matches on three states (0, 1, don't care), so it can look up masked entries like prefixes in one step","TCAM stores only exact 48-bit MAC matches, which makes lookups faster","TCAM is software memory, so it can hold unlimited entries","TCAM is only used when a packet is punted to the route processor"],0,"Ternary CAM supports a don't-care bit, so it can match prefixes and ACL masks in hardware. Binary CAM only does exact matches, such as MAC lookups.","Exam topics section 1.0 (hardware and software switching)",[null,"Exact 48-bit MAC matches are what the binary CAM does; TCAM exists precisely to match masked, non-exact entries.","TCAM is specialized hardware memory with a fixed, limited size, which is why TCAM exhaustion is a real design concern.","TCAM lookups happen in hardware for traffic that is not punted; punted packets are handled in software by the CPU instead."],2],["en13",0,1,"A router shows high CPU, and show ip cef indicates many packets are being punted. What is the most likely consequence?",["They are process switched by the CPU instead of by CEF or hardware","Those packets are dropped at ingress because CEF is disabled","Those packets are switched faster because they skip the FIB lookup","Those packets are sent to the standby supervisor for forwarding"],0,"Punted packets go to the route processor for process switching, which is slow and loads the CPU. CEF-switched traffic avoids the CPU.","Exam topics section 1.0 (hardware and software switching)",[null,"Punted packets are not dropped; they are sent to the CPU for handling, and CEF is still enabled for other traffic.","Punted packets are slower, not faster, because the CPU processes them in software instead of the fast path.","Punted traffic goes to the active route processor's CPU, not to a standby supervisor, which does not forward traffic."],2],["en14",0,1,"Which DSCP value is the standard marking for interactive voice bearer traffic?",["AF41 (34)","CS3 (24)","EF (46)","AF11 (10)"],2,"EF (46) is the conventional marking for voice bearer traffic. AF41 is commonly used for interactive video and CS3 for call signaling.","Exam topics section 1.0 (QoS)",["AF41 (34) is the usual marking for interactive video, not voice bearer traffic.","CS3 (24) is commonly used for call signaling such as SIP or SCCP, not for the voice media stream itself.",null,"AF11 (10) is a low-priority assured forwarding class often used for bulk data, not real-time voice."],1],["en15",0,2,"A team needs to run 20 Linux servers on a host with no general-purpose OS underneath, managed by vCenter. Which platform type is this?",["A type 2 hypervisor such as VirtualBox","A type 1 hypervisor such as VMware ESXi","A container runtime such as Docker Engine","A Kubernetes worker node running pods"],1,"ESXi is a bare-metal type 1 hypervisor. VirtualBox is type 2 because it runs on a host OS, and containers share the host kernel instead of running full guest operating systems.","Exam topics section 2.0 (device virtualization)",["A type 2 hypervisor like VirtualBox runs on top of a general-purpose host operating system, which the scenario rules out.",null,"Docker containers share a host OS kernel and are not managed by vCenter as full virtual machines.","A Kubernetes worker node runs containers on a host operating system, which contradicts having no OS underneath."],1],["en16",0,2,"What is the main job of a virtual switch such as the vSphere standard switch?",["Route traffic between VRFs using a separate routing table per VM","Encrypt all traffic between VMs on the same host with IPsec","Run spanning tree with the physical switches to block redundant uplinks","Connect VM vNICs to each other and to the host's physical uplinks"],3,"A virtual switch forwards frames between vNICs and physical uplinks and can tag VLANs. It does not participate in spanning tree the way a physical switch does.","Exam topics section 2.0 (device virtualization)",["Routing between VRFs is a Layer 3 function; a standard vSwitch is a Layer 2 device with no routing tables.","A vSwitch does not encrypt traffic between VMs; that would require a separate security product or IPsec in the guests.","vSphere standard switches do not run spanning tree; they avoid loops by never forwarding frames from one uplink to another.",null],1],["en17",0,2,"Two customers connected to the same branch router both use 10.1.1.0/24. They must stay isolated without buying another router. What should the engineer configure?",["Two OSPF processes that share the global routing table","A single VLAN with private VLAN isolation on the router","Policy-based routing that matches each customer's source subnet to a next hop","VRF-Lite, giving each customer's interfaces a separate routing table"],3,"VRF-Lite gives each customer an independent routing table, so overlapping subnets can coexist. Separate OSPF processes still install into the global table, where the prefixes would collide.","Exam topics section 2.0 (VRF)",["Multiple OSPF processes still install their routes into the same global routing table, where the overlapping 10.1.1.0/24 prefixes would collide.","Private VLANs isolate hosts within one Layer 2 VLAN; they do not give two customers separate routing tables for overlapping subnets.","PBR can steer traffic by source, but both customers' subnets are identical, so PBR cannot tell them apart or keep the routes separate.",null],2],["en18",0,2,"An engineer configures interface GigabitEthernet0/1 with vrf forwarding CUST-A after the IP address was already set. What happens?",["The IP address moves into the VRF automatically with no change","The IP address is removed and must be entered again","The interface stays in the global table until the router reloads","The command is rejected until the interface is shut down"],1,"Assigning an interface to a VRF clears its IP configuration, so the address must be reapplied. This is a common mistake in VRF labs.","Exam topics section 2.0 (VRF)",["The address does not carry over; IOS strips the IP configuration when the interface is moved into a VRF.",null,"The change takes effect immediately without a reload, and the interface is placed in the VRF right away.","The command is accepted on an up interface; IOS just warns that the IP address has been removed."],2],["en19",0,2,"A GRE tunnel keeps going up and down, and the log shows \"recursive routing\" messages. What is the most likely cause?",["The route to the tunnel destination is learned through the tunnel itself","The tunnel source and destination use different MTU sizes","The tunnel keepalive timer is shorter than the OSPF hello timer on the link","IPsec is not configured, so GRE refuses to stay up"],0,"Recursive routing occurs when the best path to the tunnel destination points into the tunnel. Fix it by making sure the underlay route to the destination is preferred and not advertised over the tunnel.","Exam topics section 2.0 (GRE and IPsec tunneling)",[null,"An MTU mismatch causes fragmentation or drops of large packets, not recursive routing messages and tunnel flapping.","Keepalive and OSPF hello timers are independent, and a timer difference does not create a recursive route to the tunnel destination.","GRE works without IPsec; IPsec is only needed for encryption, not to keep the tunnel up."],3],["en20",0,2,"Why is GRE often combined with IPsec for site-to-site VPNs across the internet?",["GRE adds encryption, and IPsec adds support for multicast routing updates","IPsec alone cannot carry any unicast IP traffic between sites","GRE carries multicast and routing protocol traffic, and IPsec adds encryption and integrity","GRE removes the need for IKE by exchanging keys itself"],2,"Plain IPsec (crypto map) does not carry multicast, so OSPF or EIGRP cannot run over it. GRE provides the multicast-capable tunnel and IPsec protects it.","Exam topics section 2.0 (GRE and IPsec tunneling)",["GRE provides no encryption at all; it is IPsec that encrypts, while GRE is what carries the multicast.","IPsec can carry unicast IP traffic on its own; its limitation with crypto maps is multicast and routing protocol traffic.",null,"GRE has no key exchange; IKE is still used to negotiate IPsec keys when GRE is protected with IPsec."],2],["en21",0,2,"Which IPsec mode encrypts the entire original IP packet and adds a new IP header?",["Transport mode","Tunnel mode","Aggressive mode","Main mode"],1,"Tunnel mode wraps the whole original packet in a new IP header. Transport mode protects only the payload. Main and aggressive are IKEv1 phase 1 modes, not IPsec encapsulation modes.","Exam topics section 2.0 (GRE and IPsec tunneling)",["Transport mode keeps the original IP header and protects only the payload, so it does not add a new IP header.",null,"Aggressive mode is an IKEv1 phase 1 exchange for negotiating the IKE SA, not an IPsec encapsulation mode.","Main mode is an IKEv1 phase 1 exchange that protects identities; it does not define how packets are encapsulated."],1],["en22",0,2,"In LISP, what is the difference between an EID and an RLOC?",["An EID is the router's loopback address, and an RLOC is the endpoint's MAC address","An EID is used only in the underlay, and an RLOC is used only in the overlay network","An EID is a VXLAN network ID, and an RLOC is a VRF route distinguisher","An EID identifies the endpoint; an RLOC is the router address where it is reached"],3,"LISP separates identity (EID) from location (RLOC). The map server stores EID-to-RLOC mappings, and tunnel routers encapsulate traffic toward the RLOC.","Exam topics section 2.0 (LISP and VXLAN)",["An EID is the endpoint's IP identity, not a router loopback, and an RLOC is a routable router address, not a MAC address.","It is the reverse: RLOCs are underlay addresses and EIDs live in the overlay.","VNIs belong to VXLAN and route distinguishers to MPLS VPNs; neither is a LISP EID or RLOC.",null],1],["en23",0,2,"How many Layer 2 segments can VXLAN identify, and why does that matter?",["About 4,000, because the VNI is 12 bits, the same as the VLAN ID","About 65,000, because the VNI reuses the 16-bit UDP destination port field","About 16 million, because the 24-bit VNI overcomes the 4094-VLAN limit","About 1 million, because the VNI is 20 bits like an MPLS label"],2,"The VXLAN Network Identifier is 24 bits, giving roughly 16 million segments versus 4094 usable 802.1Q VLANs. VXLAN travels in UDP (port 4789).","Exam topics section 2.0 (LISP and VXLAN)",["A 12-bit ID is the 802.1Q VLAN field; the VXLAN VNI is 24 bits, which is exactly how it escapes the ~4,000 limit.","The VNI is its own 24-bit header field; the UDP destination port is fixed at 4789 and does not identify segments.",null,"A 20-bit label is MPLS, not VXLAN; the VNI is 24 bits, giving about 16 million segments."],2],["en24",0,3,"SW1 Gi1/0/1 is set to switchport mode dynamic auto, and SW2 Gi1/0/1 is also dynamic auto. What is the result?",["It stays an access port, because neither side initiates a trunk","The link becomes a trunk, because both sides support DTP","The link goes err-disabled, because the DTP modes conflict with each other","The link becomes a trunk with native VLAN 1 only"],0,"Dynamic auto only responds to DTP requests; it never initiates. Two auto ports never negotiate a trunk. Desirable-to-auto or trunk-to-auto would form one.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: trunking)",[null,"Supporting DTP is not enough; at least one side must be desirable or trunk to actively request trunking.","Dynamic auto on both ends is not a conflict and does not err-disable anything; the link simply stays access.","No trunk forms at all, so there is no native VLAN negotiation; the link operates as an access port."],2],["en25",0,3,"A trunk passes traffic for most VLANs, but hosts in VLAN 30 cannot reach each other across it. show interfaces trunk lists VLAN 30 as active on both switches. What should be checked next?",["The allowed VLAN list on each side of the trunk","The DTP mode on the access ports in VLAN 30","The VTP domain name on the distribution switch","The EtherChannel load-balancing hash"],0,"If a VLAN exists and is active but not in the allowed list on one side, frames for it are not carried. show interfaces trunk shows allowed and forwarding VLANs separately.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: trunking)",[null,"DTP modes on access ports in VLAN 30 do not affect whether the trunk between the switches carries that VLAN.","VTP domain name mismatches affect VLAN database propagation, but the VLAN already shows as active on both switches.","The EtherChannel hash only picks which member link a flow uses; it cannot block one VLAN from crossing the trunk."],2],["en26",0,3,"Two switches are joined by four links. SW1 ports use channel-group 1 mode active and SW2 ports use channel-group 1 mode passive. What happens?",["No port channel forms, because both sides must be set to LACP active","A PAgP port channel forms, because passive is a PAgP mode","LACP forms the port channel: active initiates and passive responds","The links go err-disabled because of an EtherChannel misconfiguration"],2,"LACP active sends LACPDUs and passive responds, so active/passive works. Passive/passive fails because neither initiates. PAgP uses desirable and auto.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: EtherChannel)",["Only one side needs to be LACP active; active with passive forms a bundle, while passive with passive does not.","Passive is an LACP mode; PAgP uses desirable and auto.",null,"Active/passive is a valid LACP combination, so nothing is misconfigured and no ports are err-disabled."],2],["en27",0,3,"An EtherChannel shows one member port as suspended (s) in show etherchannel summary. What is the most likely cause?",["The port is the only one carrying traffic because of the hash algorithm","Its speed, duplex or VLAN settings do not match the other members","The port has more bandwidth than the others and was promoted to primary","The port channel interface has been shut down administratively"],1,"Members must match in speed, duplex, mode and VLAN settings; a mismatched port is suspended. A shut port channel would show all members down, not one suspended.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: EtherChannel)",["Hashing decides which member carries a flow but does not suspend any port; a suspended port carries no traffic at all.",null,"EtherChannel has no primary member, and ports with different speeds are suspended rather than promoted.","Shutting the port channel interface would take every member down, not suspend a single port."],2],["en28",0,3,"All traffic between two servers uses only one of the four links in an EtherChannel. Why?",["EtherChannel sends each packet round-robin but the other links are in standby","LACP limits a single port channel to one active link at a time","The port channel is misconfigured, because EtherChannel always splits every flow","The load-balancing hash picks one link per flow, and every packet in this flow hashes to the same link"],3,"EtherChannel hashes on addresses or ports so a given flow always uses the same member, which prevents reordering. Changing the hash to include Layer 4 ports can spread flows better.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: EtherChannel)",["EtherChannel does not use per-packet round-robin, and all bundled links actively forward traffic.","LACP can bundle up to 8 active links (with up to 8 more in hot standby), not just one.","EtherChannel never splits a single flow across links because that would cause packet reordering; this is normal behavior.",null],3],["en29",0,3,"In Rapid PVST+, which port role provides an immediate backup path to the root bridge if the root port fails?",["The backup port","The designated port","The edge port","The alternate port"],3,"An alternate port is a discarding port with an alternate path to the root, and it becomes the root port right away if the root port fails. A backup port backs up a designated port on a shared segment.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: RSTP)",["A backup port backs up a designated port on the same shared segment; it does not provide a path to the root.","A designated port forwards toward a segment away from the root; it is not a backup for the root port.","An edge port connects to end hosts and is not a path to the root bridge.",null],1],["en30",0,3,"A campus has 200 VLANs and only two distinct forwarding topologies. The team wants to reduce the number of spanning-tree instances. Which protocol should they use?",["Rapid PVST+, with one instance per VLAN","MST, mapping the VLANs into two instances","PVST+, with PortFast on every trunk","STP 802.1D, with a single common spanning tree"],1,"MST maps many VLANs to a few instances, cutting CPU and BPDU load while still allowing load sharing. 802.1D has one tree for everything and cannot load-balance VLANs.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: MST)",["Rapid PVST+ runs one instance per VLAN, which is 200 instances and the opposite of what the team wants.",null,"PVST+ is still one instance per VLAN, and PortFast belongs on edge ports, not trunks.","802.1D runs a single common tree, which reduces instances but cannot provide two different forwarding topologies for load sharing."],2],["en31",0,3,"Two MST switches share a link, but they behave as if they are in different regions. Which setting must match for them to be in the same region?",["The region name, revision number and VLAN-to-instance map","The bridge priority for instance 0 on both switches","The native VLAN and the allowed VLAN list on the trunk between them","The VTP domain name and VTP version"],0,"An MST region is defined by name, revision and the VLAN-to-instance table; any difference puts the switches in separate regions. Bridge priority affects root election, not region membership.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: MST)",[null,"Bridge priority influences root bridge election within an instance but has nothing to do with region membership.","Native and allowed VLANs affect what the trunk carries, not whether two MST switches consider themselves in the same region.","VTP settings manage VLAN database propagation; MST regions are defined only by name, revision and the VLAN-to-instance map."],2],["en32",0,3,"An access port has PortFast and BPDU guard enabled. A user plugs a small switch into it, and the port goes err-disabled. Why?",["PortFast shut the port because the switch sent more than one MAC address","Root guard shut the port because a superior BPDU arrived","BPDU guard shut the port because it received a BPDU on an edge port","Loop guard shut the port because BPDUs stopped arriving"],2,"BPDU guard err-disables an edge port that receives any BPDU. Root guard puts a port in root-inconsistent state instead, and only on superior BPDUs.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: STP features)",["PortFast only skips listening and learning; it never shuts a port, and it does not count MAC addresses (that is port security).","Root guard puts a port into root-inconsistent state rather than err-disabling it, and it is not the feature enabled on this port.",null,"Loop guard acts when BPDUs stop arriving on a non-designated port; here BPDUs started arriving on an edge port."],2],["en33",0,3,"Which feature prevents a distribution switch's downlink from becoming a root port if a newly connected switch advertises a better bridge ID?",["BPDU filter","Root guard","PortFast","UplinkFast"],1,"Root guard blocks a port (root-inconsistent) when it receives a superior BPDU, protecting the planned root placement. BPDU filter just stops sending and receiving BPDUs, which can cause loops.","Exam topics section 3.0 (Layer 2: STP features)",["BPDU filter stops sending and processing BPDUs, which can create loops; it does not block a port that hears a superior BPDU.",null,"PortFast only speeds an access port to forwarding and offers no protection against a new switch claiming root.","UplinkFast speeds recovery of an access switch's uplinks after a root port failure; it does not protect root placement."],1],["en34",0,3,"Which statement correctly contrasts EIGRP and OSPF?",["EIGRP is link-state using SPF, while OSPF is distance-vector using DUAL","Both are link-state, but only OSPF supports unequal-cost load balancing","Both use DUAL, but only EIGRP requires a backbone area","EIGRP is an advanced distance-vector protocol using DUAL, while OSPF is link-state using SPF"],3,"EIGRP uses DUAL and feasible successors; OSPF floods LSAs and runs SPF. EIGRP (not OSPF) supports unequal-cost load balancing with variance, and OSPF (not EIGRP) needs area 0.","Exam topics section 3.0 (EIGRP vs OSPF)",["This reverses the protocols: EIGRP uses DUAL and OSPF is the link-state protocol that runs SPF.","EIGRP is not link-state, and it is EIGRP (with variance), not OSPF, that supports unequal-cost load balancing.","Only EIGRP uses DUAL, and it is OSPF, not EIGRP, that requires a backbone area 0.",null],2],["en35",0,3,"R1 has two EIGRP paths to 10.5.0.0/16: feasible distance 2000 via R2 and 3000 via R3. R3's reported distance is 1500. The engineer sets variance 2. What happens?",["Only the R2 path is installed, because variance applies only to OSPF","Only the R3 path is installed, because it has the lower reported distance","Both paths are installed, because the R3 path is a feasible successor and within twice the best metric","Both paths are installed with equal traffic, because variance ignores metrics"],2,"The R3 path qualifies as a feasible successor (RD 1500 < FD 2000) and its metric 3000 is within 2 × 2000, so EIGRP installs it and shares load in proportion to the metrics.","Exam topics section 3.0 (EIGRP)",["Variance is an EIGRP feature, not OSPF-only, and with variance 2 the R3 path qualifies for installation.","The lower reported distance does not make R3 the only path; R2 has the best feasible distance and stays the successor.",null,"EIGRP unequal-cost load balancing shares traffic in proportion to the metrics, not equally."],3],["en36",0,3,"Which EIGRP feature stops the hub from querying spoke routers when it loses a route, so a hub-and-spoke network converges faster?",["Stub routing on the spoke routers","Auto-summary on the hub router","Split horizon on the spoke routers","Variance on the hub router"],0,"EIGRP stub spokes are not queried by the hub when a route is lost, which limits the query scope and avoids stuck-in-active. Split horizon is a loop-prevention rule, not a query limiter.","Exam topics section 3.0 (EIGRP)",[null,"Auto-summary summarizes at classful boundaries and does not stop the hub from sending queries to spokes.","Split horizon prevents advertising a route back out the interface it was learned on; it does not limit the query scope.","Variance enables unequal-cost load balancing and has no effect on which neighbors are queried."],2],["en37",0,3,"R1 and R2 are connected on 10.0.12.0/30 but stay in EXSTART/EXCHANGE instead of reaching FULL. Hellos, area and timers match. What is the most likely cause?",["An interface MTU mismatch between the two routers","Mismatched OSPF router IDs on the two routers","A mismatched OSPF process ID on the two routers","Mismatched reference bandwidth on the two routers"],0,"Neighbors stuck in EXSTART/EXCHANGE point to an MTU mismatch, because DBD packets are rejected. Process IDs are local and do not have to match.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF adjacency)",[null,"Duplicate router IDs cause different problems, and routers are expected to have different IDs anyway, so 'mismatched' IDs are normal.","The OSPF process ID is locally significant and does not need to match between neighbors.","Reference bandwidth changes computed costs but is not exchanged in hellos or DBDs, so it cannot block adjacency."],3],["en38",0,3,"Which OSPF area type blocks Type 5 LSAs but still allows an ASBR inside the area, using Type 7 LSAs?",["Stub area","Totally stubby area","Not-so-stubby area (NSSA)","Backbone area"],2,"An NSSA lets an internal ASBR originate external routes as Type 7 LSAs, which the ABR translates to Type 5. Stub and totally stubby areas do not allow an ASBR.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF area types)",["A stub area blocks Type 5 LSAs but does not allow an ASBR inside the area to redistribute routes.","A totally stubby area blocks Type 3, 4 and 5 LSAs and also does not permit an internal ASBR.",null,"The backbone area 0 carries Type 5 LSAs normally and cannot be configured as a stub or NSSA."],1],["en39",0,3,"An ABR connects area 0 and area 10. The engineer wants routers in area 10 to receive only a default route instead of all inter-area and external routes. What should be configured?",["area 10 nssa on the ABR only, and nothing on the area 10 routers","area 10 stub no-summary on the ABR, and area 10 stub on the area 10 routers","area 10 range on the area 10 internal routers","summary-address 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 on the ABR"],1,"A totally stubby area blocks Type 3, 4 and 5 LSAs except a default route. The no-summary keyword is needed only on the ABR, but every router in the area needs the stub flag.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF area types)",["An NSSA still carries inter-area Type 3 routes and allows Type 7, and every router in the area must agree on the area type or adjacencies fail.",null,"The area range command summarizes on an ABR; on internal routers it does nothing, and it would not replace routes with a default.","summary-address is used on an ASBR to summarize external routes, not to make an area receive only a default route."],3],["en40",0,3,"Two routers are connected point-to-point over Ethernet, and the team wants to skip DR/BDR election to speed up adjacency. What should they configure?",["ip ospf priority 255 on both interfaces","ip ospf cost 1 on both interfaces","passive-interface on both interfaces under the OSPF process","ip ospf network point-to-point on both interfaces"],3,"The point-to-point network type removes DR/BDR election and the wait timer. Priority only influences the election, and passive-interface would stop the adjacency entirely.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPF network types)",["Priority 255 on both sides still runs a DR/BDR election; it only influences who wins it.","Interface cost affects path selection but does not change the network type or remove the DR election.","passive-interface stops sending hellos, so no adjacency would form at all.",null],2],["en41",0,3,"How does OSPFv3 differ from OSPFv2 in how neighbors communicate?",["OSPFv3 uses TCP port 179 to exchange LSAs with neighbors","OSPFv3 requires a DR on every link type, including point-to-point links","OSPFv3 elects neighbors by IPv4 address and cannot use a router ID","It runs per link and uses IPv6 link-local addresses as packet sources"],3,"OSPFv3 is enabled on interfaces and uses link-local addresses for adjacencies. It still needs a 32-bit router ID, which must be set manually if no IPv4 address exists.","Exam topics section 3.0 (OSPFv3)",["TCP port 179 is BGP; OSPFv3 runs directly over IPv6 as protocol 89, just as OSPFv2 does over IPv4.","OSPFv3 keeps the same network types as OSPFv2, and point-to-point links do not elect a DR.","OSPFv3 still uses a 32-bit router ID and forms neighbors over IPv6 link-local addresses, not IPv4 addresses.",null],2],["en42",0,3,"R1 (AS 65001) and R2 (AS 65002) are directly connected. R1 shows the neighbor in Active state. What does this mean?",["The BGP session is fully up and exchanging prefixes","R1 is trying to open the TCP session, but it is not established yet","R1 has received prefixes from the neighbor but has not installed them","The neighbor has been shut down administratively"],1,"Active means BGP is repeatedly trying to establish the TCP session (port 179), often because of a wrong neighbor IP, AS number or reachability. Established is the working state.","Exam topics section 3.0 (eBGP)",["A working session is in the Established state; Active means the TCP session is not up.",null,"Received but not installed prefixes relate to an Established session and best-path or RIB issues, not the Active state.","An administratively shut neighbor shows Idle (Admin), not Active."],2],["en43",0,3,"Which BGP attribute is compared first in Cisco's best-path selection?",["Weight, with the highest value preferred","AS path length, with the shortest preferred","MED, with the lowest value preferred","Local preference, with the lowest value preferred"],0,"Cisco checks weight (local to the router, highest wins) first, then local preference (highest wins), then locally originated, then AS path length, origin and MED.","Exam topics section 3.0 (eBGP best path)",[null,"AS path length is checked only after weight, local preference and locally originated routes.","MED is compared late, after weight, local preference, locally originated, AS path and origin.","Local preference comes second, and the highest value is preferred, not the lowest."],1],["en44",0,3,"An enterprise has two ISP links on different routers and wants all outbound traffic to prefer ISP A. Both routers run iBGP. Which attribute should be set on routes received from ISP A?",["A higher weight, which is shared with iBGP peers","A lower MED, which ISP A reads to choose the entry point","A higher local preference, which iBGP peers share","A longer AS path using prepending toward ISP A"],2,"Local preference is carried inside the AS and influences outbound path choice for all routers. Weight is local to one router, and MED and prepending influence inbound traffic.","Exam topics section 3.0 (eBGP best path)",["Weight is Cisco-proprietary and local to one router; it is never sent to iBGP peers.","MED is sent to a neighboring AS to influence inbound traffic; it does not steer this enterprise's outbound choice.",null,"AS path prepending lengthens paths advertised to a neighbor to influence inbound traffic, not outbound."],3],["en45",0,3,"A branch wants traffic from the guest subnet 10.9.0.0/24 to use a cheaper internet link, while all other traffic follows the routing table. What should be configured?",["A static default route with a higher administrative distance","Policy-based routing, applied inbound on the guest-facing interface","A route map applied outbound on the WAN interface to set the next hop","OSPF cost changes on the guest-facing interface"],1,"PBR matches traffic (with an ACL in a route map) and sets the next hop, overriding the routing table for that traffic. It is applied with ip policy route-map on the ingress interface.","Exam topics section 3.0 (policy-based routing)",["A higher-AD static default is a floating backup that applies to all traffic, not just the guest subnet.",null,"Route maps for PBR are applied inbound on the ingress interface; an outbound route map on the WAN interface does not do policy routing.","OSPF cost changes affect all traffic following the route, not just packets sourced from the guest subnet."],2],["en46",0,3,"Which statement about VRRP is correct compared with HSRP?",["VRRP is Cisco proprietary and requires a virtual IP that is not on any interface","VRRP disables preemption by default, unlike HSRP","VRRP uses a standby router role instead of a backup role","VRRP is an open standard and can use a real interface address as the virtual IP"],3,"VRRP (RFC 5798) is open, preempts by default, and allows the virtual IP to be the master's own address. HSRP is Cisco proprietary and uses active and standby roles.","Exam topics section 3.0 (FHRP)",["VRRP is an open standard, and unlike HSRP it may use a real interface address as the virtual IP.","VRRP enables preemption by default; it is HSRP that has preemption disabled by default.","VRRP uses master and backup roles; active and standby are HSRP terms.",null],2],["en47",0,3,"Many inside hosts must reach the internet using one public address, 203.0.113.10, on interface Gi0/0. Which NAT configuration fits?",["Static NAT mapping each host to 203.0.113.10","Dynamic NAT with a pool that contains only 203.0.113.10 and no overload","PAT, using ip nat inside source list with the overload keyword","NAT64 translating the inside hosts to IPv6 addresses"],2,"PAT (NAT overload) lets many hosts share one address by tracking source ports. Dynamic NAT without overload allows only one inside host per public address at a time.","Exam topics section 3.0 (NAT/PAT)",["Static NAT is one-to-one; you cannot map many inside hosts to the same single address this way.","Dynamic NAT without overload lets only one inside host use 203.0.113.10 at a time.",null,"NAT64 translates between IPv6 and IPv4; it does not let many IPv4 hosts share one public IPv4 address."],2],["en48",0,3,"An engineer checks NTP on a router and sees it synchronized to a server that is stratum 2. What stratum does the router report?",["Stratum 3","Stratum 1","Stratum 2","Stratum 16"],0,"A client is one stratum higher than its source, so it reports stratum 3. Stratum 16 means unsynchronized.","Exam topics section 3.0 (NTP and PTP)",[null,"Stratum 1 is a server directly attached to a reference clock such as GPS, not a client of a stratum 2 server.","A client never reports the same stratum as its source; it is always one higher.","Stratum 16 means unsynchronized, but the router is synchronized to its server."],2],["en49",0,3,"Which application is best served by Precision Time Protocol (PTP) rather than NTP?",["Industrial and audio-video systems that need sub-microsecond clock accuracy","Setting log timestamps on routers that tolerate a few milliseconds of drift","Keeping user laptops in sync with an internet time server","Validating certificate lifetimes on web servers"],0,"PTP (IEEE 1588) uses hardware timestamping to reach sub-microsecond accuracy. NTP's millisecond accuracy is fine for logs, laptops and certificates.","Exam topics section 3.0 (NTP and PTP)",[null,"A few milliseconds of drift in log timestamps is well within what NTP delivers, so PTP is unnecessary.","Laptop clock sync over the internet is a classic NTP use case and cannot benefit from PTP hardware timestamping.","Certificate validity checks need only coarse accuracy, which NTP easily provides."],2],["en50",0,3,"A receiver on a switch port wants to join the multicast group 239.1.1.1. Which protocol does the host use to signal this to its local router?",["PIM","MSDP","IGMP","IGMP snooping"],2,"Hosts send IGMP membership reports to the local router. PIM runs between routers, and IGMP snooping is a switch feature that listens to IGMP to limit flooding.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)",["PIM runs between multicast routers to build distribution trees, not between a host and its router.","MSDP shares source information between RPs in different PIM domains; hosts never use it.",null,"IGMP snooping is a switch feature that listens to IGMP; the host itself sends IGMP, not snooping."],1],["en51",0,3,"In PIM sparse mode, what is the role of the rendezvous point (RP)?",["It floods multicast to every interface and prunes where there are no receivers","It is the shared-tree root where sources register and receivers first join","It assigns multicast group addresses to hosts that request them","It translates multicast traffic into unicast for each receiver"],1,"In PIM-SM, receivers join the shared tree toward the RP, and sources register with the RP. Flood and prune is PIM dense mode behavior.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)",["Flood and prune is PIM dense mode behavior; sparse mode sends traffic only where receivers explicitly join.",null,"Multicast group addresses are not handed out by the RP; applications or administrators choose them.","The RP forwards multicast down a shared tree; it does not convert multicast into unicast streams."],1],["en52",0,3,"Multicast traffic arrives on an interface that is not the router's path back to the source, and the router drops it. Which check caused the drop?",["The IGMP querier election","The TTL scoping check","The PIM DR election","The reverse path forwarding (RPF) check"],3,"RPF accepts multicast only on the interface the router would use to reach the source (or RP), which prevents loops. RPF failures are a common multicast troubleshooting issue.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)",["The IGMP querier election picks which router queries hosts on a LAN; it does not drop multicast data packets.","TTL scoping can limit how far multicast travels, but the drop here is due to the arrival interface, not the TTL.","The PIM DR election selects which router sends joins or registers on a LAN; it does not validate the incoming interface.",null],2],["en53",0,3,"Which multicast model lets a receiver ask for traffic from a specific source, removing the need for an RP?",["Any-source multicast with IGMPv2","PIM dense mode with IGMPv1","Bidirectional PIM with a phantom rendezvous point","Source-specific multicast (SSM) with IGMPv3"],3,"SSM uses IGMPv3 (S,G) joins so routers build a source tree directly, with no RP. IGMPv2 cannot specify a source. SSM uses the 232.0.0.0/8 range by default.","Exam topics section 3.0 (multicast)",["IGMPv2 joins are (*,G) and cannot name a source, so any-source multicast still needs an RP.","PIM dense mode floods and prunes, and IGMPv1 cannot specify a source either.","Bidirectional PIM still relies on an RP address (even a phantom one) as the root of the shared tree.",null],2],["en54",0,4,"An engineer needs debug output only for traffic from host 10.1.1.50 on a busy production router. What is the safest approach?",["Enable debug all and filter the log file afterward","Use a conditional debug limited to that host or interface","Enable debug ip packet with no ACL and send the output to the console","Reload the router with debugging enabled at startup"],1,"Conditional debugging (debug condition, or an ACL on debug ip packet) limits output so the CPU is not overwhelmed. Unfiltered debugs on a busy router can cause an outage.","Exam topics section 4.0 (diagnose network problems)",["debug all on a busy production router can flood the CPU and crash or hang it before you ever filter the log.",null,"Unfiltered debug ip packet to the console is one of the riskiest debugs, since console logging is slow and every packet generates output.","Reloading a production router causes an outage and does not limit the debug to one host."],2],["en55",0,4,"A traceroute from R1 to 192.0.2.10 shows asterisks at hop 4, but later hops respond normally. What is the most likely explanation?",["That device does not send ICMP time-exceeded replies, or rate-limits them","The destination is unreachable because a loop exists at hop 4","The hop-4 device is silently dropping all traffic headed to the destination","The traceroute TTL was set too low to reach hop 5"],0,"If later hops answer, traffic passes hop 4; that device is just not replying to expired probes. A real blocking point would stop all later replies.","Exam topics section 4.0 (diagnose network problems)",[null,"A loop would show repeating hops or TTL expirations, and later hops responding shows the destination path works.","If hop 4 dropped all traffic toward the destination, the probes to later hops would also fail.","Traceroute keeps increasing the TTL for each hop, and later hops responded, so the TTL clearly reached past hop 4."],3],["en56",0,4,"Which SNMP version adds authentication and encryption for manager-to-agent traffic?",["SNMPv2c with a read-only community string","SNMPv1 with a long community string","SNMPv3 with the authPriv security level","SNMPv2c with traps instead of polling"],2,"SNMPv3 authPriv provides both authentication and encryption. v1 and v2c send community strings in clear text.","Exam topics section 4.0 (SNMP and syslog)",["SNMPv2c community strings are sent in clear text and provide no encryption, read-only or not.","SNMPv1 also sends the community string in clear text; its length adds no encryption.",null,"Using traps changes the direction of messages but not the security model; v2c still has no authentication or encryption."],1],["en57",0,4,"A router is configured with logging trap warnings. Which messages are sent to the syslog server?",["Severity level 4 only","Severity levels 0 through 4","Severity levels 4 through 7","Severity levels 5 through 7"],1,"The trap level sends the chosen severity and everything more severe (lower number): 0 emergencies through 4 warnings.","Exam topics section 4.0 (SNMP and syslog)",["The trap level is a threshold, so more severe messages (0-3) are sent too, not only level 4.",null,"Levels 5-7 are less severe than warnings and are excluded; the range goes toward lower numbers.","Levels 5-7 (notifications, informational, debugging) are exactly the messages filtered out by a warnings threshold."],2],["en58",0,4,"The team wants to see which applications and hosts use the most WAN bandwidth, with custom key fields. What should be configured on the router?",["SPAN copying the WAN interface to a local analyzer port","IP SLA ICMP echo probes to the WAN next hop","SNMP interface counters polled every five minutes by the NMS","Flexible NetFlow with a flow record, exporter and monitor"],3,"Flexible NetFlow builds per-flow records with the key and non-key fields you choose and exports them to a collector. SNMP counters give totals, not per-flow detail.","Exam topics section 4.0 (NetFlow)",["SPAN copies raw packets to a local analyzer and does not summarize per-application usage on the router.","IP SLA probes measure reachability, latency and jitter, not which hosts and applications use bandwidth.","SNMP interface counters only give total bytes and packets per interface, with no per-flow or per-application detail.",null],2],["en59",0,4,"An IDS appliance is in the data center, three routed hops away from the switch whose port must be monitored. Which feature copies the traffic to it?",["Local SPAN, which copies to another port on the same switch","RSPAN, which carries the mirrored traffic in a dedicated VLAN across Layer 3 hops","ERSPAN, which carries the mirrored traffic in GRE across routed hops","Port security, which forwards unknown frames to a monitor port"],2,"ERSPAN uses GRE so mirrored traffic can cross routed hops. RSPAN uses a dedicated VLAN, so it works only across Layer 2 trunks.","Exam topics section 4.0 (SPAN/RSPAN/ERSPAN)",["Local SPAN can only copy to a destination port on the same switch, not to a device three routed hops away.","RSPAN uses a dedicated VLAN, so it only works across Layer 2 trunks; it cannot cross routed hops.",null,"Port security limits MAC addresses on a port and does not mirror traffic anywhere."],2],["en60",0,4,"A branch has a primary and a backup internet link. The team wants the static default route to be withdrawn if the ISP's DNS server stops responding, even while the link stays up. What should they use?",["An IP SLA ICMP echo probe tracked by the static route","A floating static route with an administrative distance of 250","SNMP traps that alert the NOC when the link goes down","A NetFlow exporter that sends flow records to the ISP"],0,"IP SLA plus object tracking removes the static route when the probe fails, even if the interface is up. A floating static route only takes over when the primary route disappears.","Exam topics section 4.0 (IP SLA)",[null,"A floating static route only takes over once the primary route is removed; it cannot detect the DNS server failure itself.","SNMP traps only notify people, and a link-down trap would not even fire because the link stays up.","NetFlow exports traffic statistics for analysis and has no effect on the routing table."],3],["en61",0,4,"Which Catalyst Center (formerly DNA Center) feature helps an engineer find the root cause of a user's poor experience over the past day?",["Assurance, which correlates device, client and app health over time","Plug and Play, which onboards new devices with day-0 configuration","Software image management, which checks devices against golden images","Template editor, which pushes configuration to groups of devices"],0,"Assurance collects telemetry and shows health scores, issues and suggested fixes, including AI-driven insights. PnP, SWIM and templates are provisioning tools.","Exam topics section 4.0 (Catalyst Center workflows)",[null,"Plug and Play automates day-0 onboarding of new devices and does not analyze user experience.","SWIM manages and upgrades software images; it does not correlate client or application health.","The template editor pushes configuration and provides no historical troubleshooting data."],2],["en62",0,4,"An engineer wants to change the hostname of a router programmatically using a YANG model over SSH (port 830). Which protocol should be used?",["RESTCONF","SNMPv3","NETCONF","gNMI over HTTP/1.1"],2,"NETCONF uses SSH (port 830) and XML-encoded YANG data. RESTCONF uses HTTPS with JSON or XML.","Exam topics section 4.0 (NETCONF and RESTCONF)",["RESTCONF uses HTTPS (TCP 443) with JSON or XML, not SSH on port 830.","SNMPv3 uses MIBs over UDP 161, not YANG models over SSH, and is rarely used for configuration changes.",null,"gNMI runs over gRPC on HTTP/2, not HTTP/1.1 or SSH port 830."],1],["en63",0,5,"An engineer wants console and VTY logins to use the local user database. Which configuration applies to the VTY lines?",["login, with a password set under the line","login local, with usernames defined by username commands","transport input none, with a local username","exec-timeout 0 0, with an enable secret"],1,"login local checks the local username database. Plain login only uses the line password, and transport input none blocks remote access entirely.","Exam topics section 5.0 (device access control)",["Plain login checks only the single password configured on the line, not the username database.",null,"transport input none blocks all remote protocols on the VTY lines, so nobody could log in remotely at all.","exec-timeout 0 0 only disables the idle timeout, and the enable secret protects privileged mode, not the VTY login method."],1],["en64",0,5,"The team wants to authorize each command an administrator enters on routers and log it centrally. Which AAA protocol is the best choice?",["RADIUS, because it encrypts the whole packet body","RADIUS, because it runs over TCP for reliability and logs each command centrally","TACACS+, because it combines authentication and authorization","TACACS+, because it separates authorization and can authorize each command"],3,"TACACS+ uses TCP 49, encrypts the whole body and separates authentication, authorization and accounting, which enables per-command authorization. RADIUS combines authentication and authorization and encrypts only the password.","Exam topics section 5.0 (AAA)",["It is TACACS+, not RADIUS, that encrypts the whole packet body; RADIUS encrypts only the password field.","RADIUS runs over UDP, not TCP, and it combines authentication and authorization, so it cannot authorize individual commands.","TACACS+ is the right protocol, but the reason is wrong: it separates authentication and authorization rather than combining them.",null],2],["en65",0,5,"A router is configured with aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local. The TACACS+ server is unreachable. What happens when an admin logs in?",["The login is denied because TACACS+ is listed first","The router allows access with no password","The router tries RADIUS before the local database","The router falls back to the local user database"],3,"The method list is tried in order, and the next method is used only when the previous one returns an error, such as an unreachable server. A reject from TACACS+ would not fall back.","Exam topics section 5.0 (AAA)",["Listing TACACS+ first does not deny the login; an unreachable server returns an error, so the router moves on to the next method.","The method list ends with local, not none, so the router still requires a local username and password.","RADIUS is not in this method list at all; only tacacs+ and then local are configured.",null],2],["en66",0,5,"Which port-based network access control method authenticates a user with credentials through a supplicant and a RADIUS server?",["MAC Authentication Bypass","802.1X","WebAuth with a guest portal","Port security with sticky MAC"],1,"802.1X uses a supplicant, the switch as authenticator and a RADIUS server. MAB authenticates by MAC address for devices without a supplicant.","Exam topics section 5.0 (network access control)",["MAB uses the device's MAC address as its identity and involves no user credentials or supplicant.",null,"WebAuth collects credentials or terms acceptance through a browser portal, not through a supplicant.","Port security with sticky MAC just learns and limits MAC addresses locally; it never contacts a RADIUS server."],1],["en67",0,5,"A printer has no 802.1X supplicant but must be placed in the correct VLAN automatically by ISE. Which method should the switch use?",["MAC Authentication Bypass (MAB)","EAP-TLS with a device certificate","Local WebAuth on the switch","Port security with a maximum of one MAC"],0,"MAB sends the device's MAC address to RADIUS as its identity, so ISE can authorize it. EAP-TLS needs a supplicant, which the printer lacks.","Exam topics section 5.0 (network access control)",[null,"EAP-TLS runs inside 802.1X and requires a supplicant to present the certificate, which the printer does not have.","WebAuth needs a person using a browser to log in, which a printer cannot do.","Port security limits MAC addresses locally but cannot have ISE assign the device to a VLAN."],2],["en68",0,5,"An extended ACL must block Telnet from 10.1.1.0/24 to server 10.2.2.10 but allow everything else. Which entry order is correct?",["permit ip any any, then deny tcp 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.2.10 eq 23","deny tcp host 10.2.2.10 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 eq 23, then permit ip any any","deny tcp 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.2.10 eq 23, then permit ip any any","deny ip 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.2.10, then permit tcp any any eq 23"],2,"ACLs are read top down and stop at the first match, so the specific deny must come before the permit. The source is the client subnet and the destination port is 23 on the server.","Exam topics section 5.0 (ACLs)",["permit ip any any matches everything first, so the Telnet deny below it is never reached.","The source and destination are reversed: this matches traffic from the server to the client subnet, not the Telnet sessions from the clients.",null,"This denies all IP traffic from the subnet to the server, not just Telnet, and the second line would only permit Telnet elsewhere."],2],["en69",0,5,"Where should an extended ACL generally be placed?",["As close to the destination of the traffic as possible","As close to the source of the traffic as possible","Only on the router's loopback interface","Only on VTY lines"],1,"Extended ACLs match source, destination and ports, so placing them near the source drops unwanted traffic early. Standard ACLs, which match only the source, go near the destination.","Exam topics section 5.0 (ACLs)",["Near the destination is the guidance for standard ACLs; an extended ACL there lets unwanted traffic cross the network before being dropped.",null,"A loopback interface does not carry transit traffic, so an ACL there would not filter traffic between networks.","An ACL on VTY lines (access-class) only controls remote management access to the device, not general traffic."],1],["en70",0,5,"A router's CPU spikes because of a flood of ICMP and SSH attempts aimed at the router itself. Which feature limits that traffic to the control plane?",["Unicast Reverse Path Forwarding","An interface ACL on the outbound direction","Storm control on the switch access ports","Control Plane Policing (CoPP)"],3,"CoPP applies a QoS policy to traffic destined to the route processor, rate-limiting it to protect the CPU. uRPF checks source addresses for spoofing, and storm control limits broadcasts on switch ports.","Exam topics section 5.0 (CoPP)",["uRPF drops packets with spoofed source addresses; it does not rate-limit legitimate-looking ICMP and SSH aimed at the router.","An outbound interface ACL filters traffic leaving the interface, and traffic to the router itself does not pass through it outbound.","Storm control limits broadcast, multicast or unknown unicast floods on switch ports, not unicast traffic addressed to a router.",null],2],["en71",0,5,"Which CoPP design is recommended?",["Police all control plane traffic to the same low rate","Drop all ICMP and SNMP traffic sent to the router","Classify traffic, allow routing protocols more and police the rest tightly","Apply one policy outbound on every physical interface instead of the control plane"],2,"CoPP classes let critical traffic such as BGP and OSPF through while limiting management and unknown traffic. A single low limit can drop routing updates and cause instability.","Exam topics section 5.0 (CoPP)",["One low rate for everything can drop routing protocol updates and cause adjacencies to flap.","Dropping all ICMP and SNMP breaks legitimate monitoring and troubleshooting; CoPP should rate-limit them instead.",null,"CoPP is applied to the control-plane interface; per-interface policies do not see all traffic punted to the CPU."],3],["en72",0,5,"A script calls a controller's REST API. Which practice best protects the API credentials and data in transit?",["Use HTTPS with token authentication and keep tokens out of the code","Use HTTP with basic authentication and store the password in the script","Use HTTPS but put the API key in the URL query string","Use HTTP but restrict the API to the management VLAN"],0,"HTTPS protects data in transit, and tokens kept in a vault or environment variable avoid hard-coded secrets. Query strings are logged by proxies and servers.","Exam topics section 5.0 (REST API security)",[null,"Plain HTTP exposes the credentials in transit, and a password stored in the script can leak through source control.","HTTPS encrypts the URL on the wire, but query strings are still written to server and proxy logs and browser history.","Restricting HTTP to a management VLAN still sends credentials unencrypted to anyone who can capture that VLAN."],2],["en73",0,5,"How does a next-generation firewall differ from a traditional stateful firewall?",["It adds application awareness, IPS and threat intelligence to stateful inspection","It filters only on source and destination ports without tracking any session state","It replaces routing, so the network needs no routers","It inspects only Layer 2 frames and cannot see IP headers"],0,"An NGFW identifies applications and users and adds IPS and malware protection. A traditional stateful firewall tracks connections but decides mostly on addresses and ports.","Exam topics section 5.0 (security design components)",[null,"Filtering only on ports without state is a basic packet filter, which is less capable than even a traditional stateful firewall.","An NGFW can route, but it does not replace the routers in an enterprise network.","An NGFW inspects up to the application layer; a device limited to Layer 2 frames would be less capable, not more."],1],["en74",0,5,"A user downloads a file that later turns out to be malicious. Which component can retroactively flag the file and quarantine it on every endpoint where it exists?",["A stateful firewall at the internet edge","An IPsec VPN concentrator","An endpoint security product with retrospective analysis, such as Cisco Secure Endpoint","802.1X port authentication on the switch"],2,"Endpoint protection with retrospective security tracks file dispositions and can act after a verdict changes. Firewalls and 802.1X do not track files on endpoints.","Exam topics section 5.0 (security design components)",["A stateful firewall inspects traffic at the time it passes and cannot find or quarantine files already sitting on endpoints.","An IPsec VPN concentrator only encrypts tunnels and has no visibility into files on endpoints.",null,"802.1X controls who connects to a port and does not track or quarantine files."],2],["en75",0,5,"How does Cisco TrustSec enforce policy?",["It builds a separate VLAN for every user and routes between them","It tags traffic with a scalable group tag (SGT) and enforces permissions with SGACLs based on group, not IP address","It encrypts every packet with IPsec between the endpoint and the firewall","It blocks unknown MAC addresses with port security"],1,"TrustSec classifies users and devices into groups (SGTs) and enforces group-to-group SGACLs, so policy does not depend on IP addressing.","Exam topics section 5.0 (TrustSec)",["TrustSec does not create a VLAN per user; its point is group policy that is independent of VLANs and IP addresses.",null,"TrustSec does not use IPsec; its link encryption option is MACsec, and enforcement is by SGACLs, not encryption.","Port security limits MAC addresses per port and is not how TrustSec classifies or enforces policy."],1],["en76",0,5,"A bank needs to encrypt traffic on the link between two switches at Layer 2, hop by hop. Which technology fits?",["IPsec tunnel mode","TLS 1.3","SSH version 2","MACsec (802.1AE)"],3,"MACsec encrypts Ethernet frames on each link at line rate. IPsec and TLS operate at Layer 3 and above.","Exam topics section 5.0 (MACsec)",["IPsec tunnel mode encrypts at Layer 3 between IP peers, not hop by hop on an Ethernet link between switches.","TLS 1.3 protects individual application sessions above Layer 4, not all frames on a link.","SSH version 2 encrypts management sessions to a device, not the user traffic crossing the switch link.",null],2],["en77",0,5,"Which switch feature blocks rogue DHCP servers on access ports?",["Dynamic ARP inspection on every trunk port","IP source guard on the DHCP server port","Storm control on the access ports","DHCP snooping, with only uplinks to the real server marked trusted"],3,"DHCP snooping drops DHCP server messages (offers and acks) on untrusted ports. DAI and IP source guard rely on the snooping binding table but do not stop rogue servers.","Exam topics section 5.0 (infrastructure security)",["DAI validates ARP packets and depends on DHCP snooping's bindings, but it does not drop rogue DHCP offers.","IP source guard filters spoofed source IPs using the binding table and does not block DHCP server messages.","Storm control limits broadcast rates and would not stop a rogue server's low-volume DHCP offers.",null],1],["en78",0,5,"A guest must accept an acceptable use policy in a browser before getting network access, with no supplicant needed. Which method is this?",["802.1X with PEAP","Web authentication (WebAuth) with a captive portal","MAC Authentication Bypass","MACsec with a pre-shared key"],1,"WebAuth redirects the user's browser to a portal to log in or accept terms. 802.1X needs a supplicant, and MAB uses the MAC address without user interaction.","Exam topics section 5.0 (network access control)",["802.1X with PEAP needs a supplicant on the device, which this scenario explicitly rules out.",null,"MAB authenticates by MAC address with no user interaction, so there is no browser page to accept a policy.","MACsec encrypts Ethernet frames on a link and does not present an acceptable use policy to users."],1],["en79",0,5,"Why should an engineer configure transport input ssh on the VTY lines?",["To reject Telnet, which sends credentials in clear text","To allow Telnet and SSH at the same time","To disable console access for remote users","To force the use of SNMPv3 for management"],0,"transport input ssh allows only SSH on the VTY lines. SSH also needs a hostname, domain name and RSA key pair.","Exam topics section 5.0 (device access control)",[null,"Allowing both would be transport input ssh telnet (or all); transport input ssh permits only SSH.","transport input applies to VTY lines only and has no effect on the console port.","transport input controls remote login protocols; it has nothing to do with SNMP versions."],1],["en80",0,5,"Which statement correctly describes the difference between RADIUS and TACACS+ transport?",["RADIUS uses TCP port 49, and TACACS+ uses UDP 1812","Both use UDP, but only TACACS+ encrypts the password","RADIUS uses UDP (1812/1813), and TACACS+ uses TCP port 49","Both use TCP, but only RADIUS encrypts the whole payload"],2,"RADIUS runs over UDP and encrypts only the password field; TACACS+ runs over TCP 49 and encrypts the whole body.","Exam topics section 5.0 (AAA)",["The ports are swapped: TCP 49 is TACACS+ and UDP 1812 is RADIUS.","TACACS+ uses TCP, not UDP, and it is RADIUS that encrypts only the password while TACACS+ encrypts the whole body.",null,"RADIUS uses UDP, not TCP, and it encrypts only the password; TACACS+ is the one that encrypts the whole payload."],1],["en81",0,6,"A Python script receives a JSON response and needs the value \"up\" from {\"interface\": {\"name\": \"Gi1\", \"status\": \"up\"}}. The response is loaded into the variable data. Which expression returns it?",["data.interface.status()","data[\"interface\"][\"status\"]","data[0][1]","data[\"status\"][\"interface\"]"],1,"json.loads returns nested dictionaries, so you index by key from the outside in. Dictionaries are not indexed by position.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Python and JSON)",["Python dictionaries from json.loads do not support attribute access, and status is a string value, not a method to call.",null,"A dictionary is indexed by key, not by position, so data[0] raises a KeyError.","The keys are in the wrong order: status is nested inside interface, not the other way around."],2],["en82",0,6,"Which JSON snippet is valid?",["{'vlans': [10, 20], 'enabled': True}","{\"vlans\": (10, 20), \"enabled\": true}","{vlans: [10, 20], enabled: true}","{\"vlans\": [10, 20], \"enabled\": true}"],3,"JSON requires double-quoted keys and strings, square brackets for arrays and lowercase true/false. Single quotes and True are Python syntax, not JSON.","Exam topics section 6.0 (JSON)",["JSON requires double quotes, and True with a capital T is Python syntax; JSON uses lowercase true.","Parentheses are a Python tuple; JSON arrays must use square brackets.","JSON object keys must be double-quoted strings; unquoted keys are JavaScript object syntax, not valid JSON.",null],2],["en83",0,6,"What does a YANG model define?",["The transport and port a management protocol uses","The encryption used between a controller and its devices","The structure and data types of configuration and operational data that a device exposes","The Python library used to send API requests"],2,"YANG is a data modeling language: containers, lists, leaves and their types. NETCONF and RESTCONF carry data that follows YANG models.","Exam topics section 6.0 (YANG)",["The transport and port come from the protocol, such as NETCONF over SSH 830 or RESTCONF over HTTPS, not from YANG.","Encryption is provided by SSH or TLS underneath NETCONF or RESTCONF; YANG only describes data.",null,"YANG is language-neutral; a Python library such as ncclient or requests is chosen separately by the script author."],1],["en84",0,6,"A RESTCONF request to update an interface returns HTTP 401. What does this indicate?",["The request lacked valid authentication credentials","The resource was created successfully","The URL path does not exist on the device","The server had an internal error"],0,"401 Unauthorized means authentication is missing or invalid. 201 is Created, 404 is Not Found and 500 is an internal server error.","Exam topics section 6.0 (REST response codes)",[null,"A successful creation returns 201 Created, not 401.","A path that does not exist returns 404 Not Found, not 401.","An internal server error is reported as 500, a server-side 5xx code, not the client-side 401."],1],["en85",0,6,"Which HTTP method should a script use to retrieve the device inventory from the Catalyst Center API without changing anything?",["GET","POST","PUT","DELETE"],0,"GET reads a resource without side effects. POST creates, PUT replaces and DELETE removes.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Catalyst Center APIs)",[null,"POST creates a new resource or triggers an action, so it is not the read-only choice.","PUT replaces a resource with the data sent, which changes the configuration.","DELETE removes a resource, which is the opposite of a read-only request."],1],["en86",0,6,"Before calling other Catalyst Center Intent API endpoints, what must a script usually do first?",["Send a GET to the device list with no headers","Open an SSH session to the controller on port 830","Send a POST to the authentication endpoint with basic credentials to obtain a token","Register the script's MAC address in ISE"],2,"The script POSTs to the token endpoint and then sends the token in the X-Auth-Token header on later calls.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Catalyst Center APIs)",["Without a token in the X-Auth-Token header, a GET to the device list is rejected with 401 Unauthorized.","Port 830 is NETCONF over SSH; the Catalyst Center Intent API is a REST API over HTTPS.",null,"ISE MAC registration is for network access control of endpoints, not for authenticating API calls to Catalyst Center."],2],["en87",0,6,"An engineer needs a script to pull the list of WAN edge devices and their tunnel health across all branches. Which API should the script target?",["The SD-WAN Validator (formerly vBond) CLI","The SD-WAN Manager (formerly vManage) REST API","The WAN Edge local NETCONF interface on each branch","The SNMP MIB on the SD-WAN Controller"],1,"SD-WAN Manager is the central management point and exposes REST APIs for inventory, monitoring and configuration across the fabric. Querying each edge separately does not scale.","Exam topics section 6.0 (SD-WAN Manager APIs)",["The Validator handles device authentication and onboarding and does not provide a central inventory and health API.",null,"Querying each WAN Edge separately over NETCONF does not scale and lacks the fabric-wide view the Manager provides.","The Controllers run OMP and policy; an SNMP MIB there would not give the full per-branch inventory and tunnel health view."],2],["en88",0,6,"An engineer wants a router to automatically save a copy of its running configuration to a TFTP server whenever someone changes it, using only on-box features. What should be used?",["An Ansible playbook run from a cron job on a server","A NetFlow monitor that exports configuration records","An IP SLA probe that tracks the TFTP server","An EEM applet that triggers on the configuration change syslog message"],3,"Embedded Event Manager runs on the device and can react to syslog patterns such as the configuration change message, then run CLI actions. Ansible runs off-box.","Exam topics section 6.0 (EEM)",["Ansible from a cron job on a server is off-box and runs on a schedule, not immediately when the config changes.","NetFlow exports traffic flow records and has no way to export or back up configurations.","IP SLA measures reachability to a target; tracking the TFTP server does not copy any configuration.",null],3],["en89",0,6,"Which configuration management tool is agentless and connects to network devices over SSH using YAML playbooks?",["Puppet","Chef","SaltStack with minions","Ansible"],3,"Ansible is agentless and uses SSH (or APIs) with YAML playbooks. Puppet and Chef traditionally use agents that pull from a master server, and Salt uses minions.","Exam topics section 6.0 (orchestration tools)",["Puppet traditionally uses an agent on each node pulling from a Puppet server, and its manifests are not YAML playbooks.","Chef uses agents (chef-client) and Ruby-based recipes and cookbooks, not agentless YAML playbooks.","SaltStack with minions is agent-based; the minion is the agent running on each managed node.",null],1],["en90",0,6,"What is a key difference between agent-based and agentless orchestration?",["Agent-based tools use only SSH, while agentless tools need a local daemon","Agent-based tools install software on each managed node, while agentless tools use existing access such as SSH or APIs","Agentless tools cannot manage network devices, only servers","Agent-based tools never use a central server for configuration"],1,"Agents run on the managed node and usually pull configuration from a server. Agentless tools push over existing protocols, which suits network devices where agents may not be installable.","Exam topics section 6.0 (orchestration tools)",["This is reversed: agentless tools use SSH or APIs, while agent-based tools run a local daemon.",null,"Agentless tools such as Ansible are widely used to manage network devices.","Agent-based tools like Puppet and Chef typically rely on a central server that agents pull configuration from."],1],["en91",0,6,"A Python script should retry a REST call only if the device returns status 503. Which construct handles this cleanly?",["A while loop that checks response.status_code == 503 and stops after a set number of attempts","A for loop over the JSON keys of the response body","A single if statement that exits when the status is 200","A try block that catches KeyError from the dictionary"],0,"A bounded loop that checks the status code retries safely without looping forever. KeyError handling is about missing dictionary keys, not HTTP status.","Exam topics section 6.0 (Python)",[null,"Looping over JSON keys inspects the response body; it does not repeat the request based on its status code.","A single if runs once and cannot retry; it also does nothing specific for a 503 response.","KeyError is raised for missing dictionary keys, not for HTTP status codes like 503."],3],["en92",0,6,"How can AI-driven analytics in Catalyst Center improve operations compared with fixed static thresholds?",["It removes the need to collect any telemetry from network devices","It guarantees that no configuration change can cause an outage","It learns a baseline of normal behavior for each site and flags anomalies that deviate from it","It replaces routing protocols with machine learning path selection"],2,"AI/ML-based assurance builds dynamic baselines and highlights deviations and likely root causes, reducing noise from static thresholds. It still depends on telemetry and does not replace routing.","Exam topics section 6.0 (AI in network operations)",["AI analytics depends on telemetry; without data there is nothing to baseline or analyze.","No analytics tool can guarantee changes will not cause outages; AI helps detect and explain problems, not prevent all of them.",null,"Catalyst Center AI analytics works on assurance data and does not replace routing protocols for path selection."],2],["en93",0,6,"A team plans to let an AI assistant propose and push configuration changes. Which control is most important?",["Giving the assistant full admin access so it can fix issues quickly","Human approval and least-privilege access, with changes logged and reversible","Disabling logging so the assistant's changes do not fill syslog","Allowing the assistant to change production without a change window"],1,"AI output can be wrong, so changes need review, scoped permissions, audit logs and rollback. Unrestricted access removes the safety net.","Exam topics section 6.0 (AI in network operations)",["Full admin access removes the safety net; a wrong suggestion could take down the network with nothing to stop it.",null,"Disabling logging removes the audit trail needed to review and roll back the assistant's changes.","Skipping change windows puts unreviewed changes into production at any time, increasing the risk of outages."],3],["x1",0,1,"During bursts, a WAN router's output queue fills and tail drop hits many TCP flows at the same moment, so they all back off and ramp up together (global synchronization). Which QoS tool is designed to reduce this?",["LLQ with a strict priority queue for all TCP traffic","Policing TCP traffic to a fixed rate at ingress","WRED, dropping selected packets early before the queue is full","A larger hardware transmit ring on the interface so the output queue never fills up"],2,"WRED randomly drops some packets (more from lower-priority markings) as the average queue depth rises, so only some TCP flows slow down at a time. LLQ is tempting, but a priority queue for all TCP would starve other traffic and does nothing about synchronized tail drop.","Domain 1: QoS, WRED",["A strict priority queue for all TCP would starve other classes and does not stop synchronized tail drop.","Policing at ingress caps a rate but does not manage queue depth or randomize drops on the congested egress.",null,"A bigger transmit ring only adds delay; when it fills, tail drop and synchronization still happen."],2],["x2",0,1,"An access switch sends voice frames to a distribution switch over an 802.1Q trunk with a CoS value of 5. Where is CoS carried, and what happens to it when the packet is routed to another subnet?",["In the 802.1Q tag's priority bits; it is lost when the Layer 2 header is rewritten","In the IP header's ToS byte; it survives unchanged across every routed hop to the destination","In the 802.1Q VLAN ID field; the router copies it into the next tag","In the Ethernet frame check sequence; each hop recalculates it"],0,"CoS is the 3-bit priority (PCP) field in the 802.1Q tag, so it exists only on tagged links and disappears when a router builds a new frame. The ToS byte option describes DSCP, which is the Layer 3 marking that does survive routing.","Domain 1: QoS marking, CoS vs DSCP",[null,"The ToS byte holds DSCP, a Layer 3 marking; CoS lives in the Layer 2 802.1Q tag.","The VLAN ID field identifies the VLAN; priority uses the separate 3-bit PCP field.","The FCS is an error check over the frame and carries no QoS marking."],2],["x3",0,1,"A provider must enforce a 100 Mb/s contract on traffic arriving from a customer on its edge router's inbound interface. Which tool fits, and why?",["Shaping, which buffers the excess traffic as it arrives inbound","Policing, which can drop or re-mark the excess as it arrives","WRED, which re-marks the excess traffic as EF before queuing","CBWFQ, which guarantees the customer minimum bandwidth inbound"],1,"Policing can be applied inbound and immediately drops or re-marks traffic that exceeds the configured rate. Shaping is tempting but it works on egress queues, delaying excess traffic rather than enforcing a limit as packets arrive.","Domain 1: QoS, policing vs shaping",["Shaping works on output queues and delays traffic; it is not how a provider enforces an inbound limit.",null,"WRED manages queue depth by early drops; it does not enforce a rate or re-mark to EF.","CBWFQ allocates bandwidth among classes on egress queues, not a rate cap on inbound traffic."],2],["x4",0,1,"R1 is the HSRP active router with priority 110 and R2 has priority 100. R1 reloads, R2 takes over, and when R1 comes back it stays in standby. What is missing from R1's configuration?",["A priority above 150, because 110 is not high enough to win","A matching HSRP version on the two routers","An object track on R2's uplink interface","The standby preempt command on R1"],3,"HSRP does not take back the active role based on priority alone; preemption must be enabled on the higher-priority router. Raising the priority is tempting, but without preempt even a much higher priority leaves R1 in standby until R2 fails.","Domain 1: High availability, FHRP preemption",["Priority 110 already beats 100; without preempt, no priority value makes R1 take over.","Mismatched versions would stop the routers from seeing each other at all, not cause this behavior.","Tracking lowers R2's priority on an uplink failure, but R1 still needs preempt to take over.",null],2],["x5",0,1,"In a Cisco SD-Access fabric, which node role connects the fabric to networks outside it, such as the data center, WAN or internet?",["Border node","Edge node","Control plane node","Intermediate node"],0,"Border nodes are the fabric's gateway to external networks and hand traffic between the fabric and outside routing domains. The edge node is tempting, but it connects endpoints such as users and access points, not external networks.","Domain 1: SD-Access fabric roles",[null,"Edge nodes connect endpoints to the fabric; they are not the exit to outside networks.","The control plane node is the LISP map server and resolver that tracks endpoints; it does not route traffic out.","Intermediate nodes are underlay routers that just forward fabric traffic; they have no fabric edge role."],1],["x6",0,1,"A company plans to replace its MPLS WAN with SD-WAN running over broadband internet circuits. Which is a realistic limitation to plan for?",["SD-WAN cannot use broadband internet circuits as a transport","Every branch must buy its internet service from the same ISP","Internet underlays have no provider SLA, so quality still rests on them","SD-WAN cannot encrypt the traffic that travels between branch sites over the internet"],2,"SD-WAN can steer traffic around a bad path, but it cannot make an internet circuit meet an SLA the provider never offered. The other choices are false: SD-WAN mixes transports and ISPs freely and builds encrypted IPsec tunnels between WAN Edges.","Domain 1: SD-WAN benefits and limitations",["Using broadband and other internet circuits is one of SD-WAN's main selling points.","SD-WAN is transport-independent; branches can mix any ISPs.",null,"WAN Edges build encrypted IPsec tunnels between sites by default."],2],["x7",0,1,"A Layer 2 switch receives a frame in VLAN 20 whose destination MAC address it has never seen. What does it do?",["Drops the frame after a TCAM lookup miss","Floods it within VLAN 20 after a CAM table miss","Punts it to the CPU for ARP resolution after a FIB miss","Sends it only out the root port, per the STP table"],1,"An unknown unicast destination is a CAM (MAC address) table miss, so the switch floods the frame out every port in that VLAN except the one it came in on. The ARP choice is tempting, but ARP is a Layer 3 function and a Layer 2 switch simply floods.","Domain 1: CAM table",["TCAM holds ACL and QoS entries; a MAC miss does not cause a drop.",null,"A Layer 2 switch does not route or ARP for frames it is switching; it floods unknown unicast.","STP decides which ports forward, but unknown unicast is flooded out all forwarding ports in the VLAN."],1],["x8",0,1,"Which statement about the RIB and the CEF FIB on an IOS router is correct?",["The RIB is derived from the FIB after routing protocols fill it","The FIB contains only directly connected and static routes","The RIB stores the Layer 2 rewrite used for each packet","The FIB is built from the RIB with recursion pre-resolved"],3,"Routing protocols populate the RIB, and CEF builds the FIB from it with recursive next hops already resolved so each packet needs a single lookup. The Layer 2 rewrite choice is wrong because that data lives in the CEF adjacency table, not the RIB.","Domain 1: Hardware and software switching, RIB and FIB",["The direction is reversed: routing protocols feed the RIB, and the FIB is built from it.","The FIB mirrors every usable route in the RIB, including dynamic routes.","Layer 2 rewrite data is stored in the CEF adjacency table, not the RIB.",null],2],["x9",0,1,"A manufacturer's control application needs very low latency to machines on the plant floor. Leadership also wants email and CRM without buying more servers. Which deployment approach fits best?",["Hybrid: keep the control app on site and move email and CRM to SaaS","Public cloud for everything, since cloud hosting always lowers latency","On premises for everything, since SaaS cannot be secured properly","A private cloud in the provider's region for the control application"],0,"A hybrid model keeps the latency-sensitive workload close to the machines while offloading commodity apps to SaaS. Moving everything to public cloud is tempting for cost, but a distant data center adds WAN latency the control app cannot tolerate.","Domain 1: Cloud vs on-premises design",[null,"Cloud hosting adds distance and WAN latency for a plant-floor control app.","SaaS can be secured; keeping everything on site ignores the no-new-servers goal.","A provider-region private cloud is still remote, so it does not fix the latency need."],2],["x10",0,2,"A team wants to run dozens of instances of a small web service on one Linux host, with fast startup and little overhead. Why are containers usually chosen over virtual machines here?",["Each container carries its own guest kernel, giving stronger isolation","Containers share the host kernel, so they start fast and use less","Containers need a type 1 hypervisor that replaces the host OS","Containers can run any OS, such as Windows on a Linux kernel"],1,"Containers package the app and its libraries but share the host OS kernel, which makes them lightweight and quick to start. The guest-kernel choice describes virtual machines, and it is the reason VMs are heavier, not lighter.","Domain 2: Containers vs virtual machines",["Containers do not carry their own kernel; separate kernels are what VMs have.",null,"Containers run on a normal host OS; a type 1 hypervisor is for VMs.","Containers share the host kernel, so they cannot run a different OS kernel such as Windows on Linux."],1],["x11",0,2,"Users can ping servers across a GRE tunnel, but large web pages and file transfers hang. The physical links have a 1500-byte MTU. What is the recommended fix on the tunnel interfaces?",["Send GRE keepalives more often so the tunnel stays up","Change the tunnel source to a loopback interface","Set ip mtu 1400 and ip tcp adjust-mss 1360","Use IPsec transport mode so no extra header is added"],2,"GRE adds headers, so full-size packets exceed the path MTU; lowering the tunnel IP MTU and clamping TCP MSS keeps TCP segments small enough to avoid fragmentation or black-holing. Keepalives are tempting but only detect a dead tunnel; this tunnel is up and small pings succeed.","Domain 2: GRE tunnels, MTU and MSS",["Keepalives detect a dead tunnel; this tunnel is up and small packets already work.","A loopback source improves resilience but does not change packet size.",null,"Transport mode still adds an ESP header and trailer, so it adds overhead rather than removing the cause of the MTU problem."],3],["x12",0,2,"In an IKEv1 site-to-site IPsec VPN, how does Phase 1 differ from Phase 2?",["Phase 1 negotiates the IPsec SAs that carry user data; Phase 2 then authenticates the peers","Phase 1 builds the GRE tunnel, and Phase 2 adds encryption to it","Both phases negotiate the same SA, and Phase 2 only rekeys it","Phase 1 authenticates peers and builds the ISAKMP SA; Phase 2 sets up IPsec SAs"],3,"Phase 1 authenticates the peers and creates a protected ISAKMP (IKE) SA, and Phase 2 runs inside it to negotiate the IPsec SAs that protect user traffic. The first option reverses the two phases, which is the classic trap.","Domain 2: IPsec, IKE phases",["This reverses the phases; IPsec SAs for data are negotiated in Phase 2.","IKE does not build GRE; GRE is configured separately and optionally protected by IPsec.","Phase 1 and Phase 2 create different SAs with different jobs.",null],2],["x13",0,2,"Two sites must run OSPF across an IPsec VPN, and the engineer wants a routable interface with no crypto ACL to maintain. What should be configured?",["A static VTI using tunnel mode ipsec ipv4 and tunnel protection","A crypto map on the physical interface with a crypto ACL","IPsec transport mode through a crypto map and no tunnel interface","An SSL VPN client session running on each of the routers"],0,"A virtual tunnel interface is a real routed interface, so OSPF can form adjacencies over it and traffic is encrypted by routing into the tunnel rather than by matching a crypto ACL. A crypto map is tempting, but it needs a crypto ACL and does not natively carry multicast routing protocol hellos.","Domain 2: Virtual tunnel interfaces",[null,"A crypto map requires a crypto ACL, which the team wants to avoid, and has no routable interface.","Transport mode with a crypto map still needs a crypto ACL and gives no interface for OSPF.","Routers do not use SSL VPN clients for site-to-site routing adjacencies."],3],["x14",0,2,"A LISP ITR must forward traffic to an EID it has no mapping for. Where does it send its Map-Request?",["To the map server, where ETRs look up their own registrations","To the map resolver, which relays it toward the authoritative ETR","To every ETR in the LISP site by broadcast","To the proxy ETR, which holds every EID-to-RLOC mapping"],1,"ITRs send Map-Requests to a map resolver, which forwards them through the mapping system to the map server and on to the ETR that registered the EID. The map server is tempting, but it is where ETRs register mappings, not where ITRs send queries.","Domain 2: LISP map server and map resolver",["ETRs register with the map server; ITRs send requests to the map resolver.",null,"LISP does not broadcast Map-Requests to ETRs.","A proxy ETR helps non-LISP sites reach LISP sites; it is not the mapping database."],2],["x15",0,2,"Which statement correctly describes how VXLAN carries traffic between two VTEPs?",["It carries the frame inside GRE with a 12-bit VLAN ID","The VTEP rewrites the source MAC and forwards at Layer 2","It wraps the Ethernet frame in a VXLAN header, UDP and outer IP","It encrypts the whole frame with IPsec before the underlay network sends it on"],2,"VXLAN is MAC-in-UDP: the original frame gets a VXLAN header with a 24-bit VNI, then a UDP header (IANA port 4789) and an outer IP header between VTEP addresses. GRE with a VLAN ID is tempting but describes a different encapsulation and the old 12-bit limit VXLAN was built to escape.","Domain 2: VXLAN encapsulation",["VXLAN uses UDP, not GRE, and a 24-bit VNI rather than a 12-bit VLAN ID.","VXLAN tunnels the original frame unchanged; it does not rewrite MACs.",null,"VXLAN provides segmentation, not encryption."],1],["x16",0,3,"A security review requires that a user-facing switch port never become a trunk, even if the attached device sends DTP frames. Which configuration meets this?",["switchport mode dynamic desirable","switchport mode trunk together with switchport nonegotiate","switchport mode dynamic auto","switchport mode access with switchport nonegotiate"],3,"Static access mode keeps the port from trunking, and nonegotiate stops it from sending or acting on DTP. The trunk-with-nonegotiate option is tempting because it disables DTP, but it forces the port to be a trunk, which is the opposite of the requirement.","Domain 3: DTP and trunking",["Dynamic desirable actively tries to form a trunk.","This forces the port to be a trunk, the opposite of the requirement.","Dynamic auto becomes a trunk if the other side is desirable or trunk.",null],3],["x17",0,3,"To reduce the risk of double-tagging VLAN hopping across 802.1Q trunks, what is the recommended native VLAN practice?",["Use an unused VLAN as native and assign it to no access ports","Keep VLAN 1 as the native VLAN on every trunk and tag nothing else on it","Enable PortFast on all trunk ports","Allow every VLAN on every trunk"],0,"Double tagging works because the outer tag matching the native VLAN is stripped, so a native VLAN that no user port belongs to removes the attack path. Keeping VLAN 1 as native is the default and is exactly what the attack relies on.","Domain 3: Native VLAN",[null,"VLAN 1 as native is the default that double tagging exploits.","PortFast is for edge ports and has nothing to do with VLAN tagging.","Allowing all VLANs widens exposure and does not address the native VLAN."],1],["x18",0,3,"SW1's ports use channel-group 5 mode auto, and SW2's ports use channel-group 5 mode auto. What is the result?",["PAgP forms the bundle because both ends support PAgP","No EtherChannel forms, since auto only answers and never starts","LACP is negotiated instead, because PAgP automatically falls back to LACP when it fails","The ports err-disable because of a mode mismatch"],1,"PAgP auto is passive: it responds to PAgP packets but never sends them, so two auto ends never negotiate and the links stay individual. The first option is tempting, but both ends supporting PAgP is not enough without one side in desirable mode.","Domain 3: EtherChannel, PAgP modes",["Both ends running PAgP is not enough when neither sends PAgP packets.",null,"PAgP and LACP are separate; one does not fall back to the other.","Auto-auto is a valid config that simply does not bundle; ports stay up individually."],2],["x19",0,3,"An alternate port on an access switch stops receiving BPDUs because of a unidirectional fiber fault. It moves to designated and forwarding, and a loop forms. Which feature would have prevented this?",["BPDU guard","Root guard","Loop guard","PortFast"],2,"Loop guard puts a non-designated port that stops receiving BPDUs into loop-inconsistent (blocking) instead of letting it become designated. Root guard is tempting but acts when a port receives superior BPDUs, not when BPDUs stop arriving.","Domain 3: Spanning tree, loop guard",["BPDU guard shuts ports that receive BPDUs; here BPDUs stopped arriving.","Root guard blocks superior BPDUs; it does not react to missing BPDUs.",null,"PortFast skips listening and learning, which would make the loop worse."],2],["x20",0,3,"The engineer wants the core switch CORE1 to be the root bridge for VLAN 10, with CORE2 as a predictable backup root. Which configuration achieves this?",["spanning-tree portfast default on both core switches","spanning-tree vlan 10 cost 1 on every CORE1 uplink and a higher cost on every CORE2 uplink","Replace CORE1 with a switch that has a lower MAC address","spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 4096 on CORE1 and 8192 on CORE2"],3,"The lowest bridge priority wins root, so explicit low priorities make CORE1 root and CORE2 next in line regardless of MAC addresses. Changing port cost is tempting, but cost affects which ports forward toward the root, not which switch is elected root.","Domain 3: Spanning tree, root placement",["PortFast affects edge ports, not root election.","Port cost changes path selection toward the root, not which switch is root.","Relying on MAC address is unpredictable and not a design practice.",null],2],["x21",0,3,"Why can RSTP bring a point-to-point link between two switches to forwarding in about a second instead of waiting on timers?",["The proposal/agreement handshake syncs downstream ports first","RSTP removes the blocking state, so every port starts out forwarding immediately","RSTP shortens max age to two seconds on every link","BPDU guard lets designated ports skip learning"],0,"On point-to-point links, a designated port sends a proposal, the neighbor syncs (blocks its non-edge ports) and returns an agreement, so forwarding starts without timer-based listening and learning. RSTP still blocks ports (discarding state), so the second option is wrong.","Domain 3: RSTP rapid convergence",[null,"RSTP still has a discarding state; ports do not start forwarding.","RSTP does not set max age to two seconds; its speed comes from the handshake.","BPDU guard shuts ports that get BPDUs; it does not speed designated port transitions."],3],["x22",0,3,"With default K values, which values does EIGRP use to calculate its composite metric?",["Bandwidth, delay, load and reliability of each link","Minimum bandwidth on the path and total delay","Hop count and the slowest link bandwidth","An interface cost based on reference bandwidth"],1,"By default only K1 and K3 are nonzero, so the metric uses the slowest bandwidth along the path and the sum of delays. Load and reliability are part of the formula, which makes the first option tempting, but their K values are 0 by default.","Domain 3: EIGRP metric",["Load and reliability have K values of 0 by default, so they are not used.",null,"EIGRP does not use hop count in its metric.","Interface cost from reference bandwidth is how OSPF calculates its metric."],1],["x23",0,3,"R2 has 10.1.0.0/24 through 10.1.3.0/24 in EIGRP AS 100. The engineer enters ip summary-address eigrp 100 10.1.0.0 255.255.252.0 on R2's interface toward R1. What does R2 now do?",["Sends R1 the summary plus all four /24 routes","Advertises the /22 summary out every EIGRP interface on R2, not just toward R1","Sends R1 only 10.1.0.0/22 and adds a local route to Null0","Removes the four /24 routes from its own routing table"],2,"Interface summarization suppresses the component routes on that interface only and creates a summary route to Null0 on R2 to prevent loops. The every-interface option is wrong because EIGRP summaries are configured per interface.","Domain 3: EIGRP summarization",["Summarization suppresses the component /24s on that interface.","EIGRP summaries are per interface, not global.",null,"R2 keeps its /24 routes; only what it advertises to R1 changes."],3],["x24",0,3,"Three OSPF routers on one Ethernet segment start at the same time. R1 has priority 1 and RID 1.1.1.1, R2 has priority 1 and RID 2.2.2.2, and R3 has priority 0 and RID 9.9.9.9. Which router becomes the DR?",["R3, because it has the highest router ID","R1, because it has the lowest router ID","None, because one router has priority 0","R2, the highest RID among eligible routers"],3,"Priority 0 makes R3 ineligible, so among the tied priority-1 routers the highest router ID, R2, becomes DR and R1 becomes BDR. Picking R3 for its RID is the trap; the router ID is only a tiebreaker among eligible routers.","Domain 3: OSPF DR election",["Priority 0 makes R3 ineligible no matter its router ID.","The higher router ID wins the tie, not the lower one.","Priority 0 removes only that router from election; the others still elect.",null],2],["x25",0,3,"ABR R1 sits between area 0 and area 5. The engineer wants R1 to advertise 172.16.0.0/16 into area 0 instead of the individual area 5 subnets. Which command does this?",["area 5 range 172.16.0.0 255.255.0.0 on R1","summary-address 172.16.0.0 255.255.0.0 on R1","ip summary-address ospf on the area 0 interface","area 0 range 172.16.0.0 255.255.0.0 on an area 5 router"],0,"OSPF inter-area summarization is done on the ABR with area <source-area> range. The summary-address command is tempting, but in OSPF it summarizes external routes on an ASBR, not inter-area routes.","Domain 3: OSPF summarization",[null,"summary-address summarizes external routes on an ASBR.","ip summary-address ospf is not how OSPF summarizes inter-area routes.","The range must be configured on the ABR for the area the routes come from."],2],["x26",0,3,"An ABR connects area 0 and area 20. One specific inter-area prefix must be kept out of area 20 while all other routes still flow. What should the engineer configure?",["distribute-list in on an internal router in area 20","area 20 filter-list prefix with direction in on the ABR","Make area 20 a totally stubby area","passive-interface on the ABR's area 20 interface"],1,"A filter-list on the ABR filters Type 3 LSAs as they are created for the area, so the prefix never enters area 20's database. A distribute-list is tempting, but it only keeps the route out of the local routing table; the LSA still floods through the area.","Domain 3: OSPF route filtering",["A distribute-list filters only the local routing table; the LSA still floods.",null,"Totally stubby blocks all inter-area routes, not just one prefix.","passive-interface stops adjacencies, cutting area 20 off entirely."],3],["x27",0,3,"Four OSPF routers share a broadcast segment. R3 and R4, both DROTHERs, show each other in the 2-WAY state. What does this indicate?",["The hello and dead timers do not match","The interface MTU differs between them","Normal behavior; DROTHERs go FULL only with DR and BDR","The two routers have duplicate router IDs"],2,"On broadcast networks DROTHERs stop at 2-WAY with each other and form FULL adjacencies only with the DR and BDR. An MTU mismatch is tempting, but it causes a stall in EXSTART or EXCHANGE, not a stable 2-WAY state.","Domain 3: OSPF neighbor states",["Mismatched timers prevent the neighbor relationship from forming at all.","An MTU mismatch stalls routers in EXSTART or EXCHANGE, not 2-WAY.",null,"Duplicate router IDs cause errors and flapping, not a steady 2-WAY state."],2],["x28",0,3,"R1 in AS 65001 peers with directly connected R2. R2 is in AS 65002, but R1 was configured with neighbor 10.0.12.2 remote-as 65020. What happens to the session?",["It reaches Established but no prefixes are exchanged","It stays in Connect because TCP port 179 is filtered","Routes are received but marked as invalid","The OPEN is rejected with a bad peer AS notification"],3,"BGP checks the AS number in the peer's OPEN message; a mismatch triggers a NOTIFICATION and the session never reaches Established. Connect is tempting, but TCP can open fine here; the failure happens at OPEN.","Domain 3: BGP neighbor states",["The session cannot reach Established with a wrong AS number.","TCP is not filtered in this scenario; the problem is the AS in the OPEN.","No routes are exchanged because the session never comes up.",null],3],["x29",0,3,"An enterprise has links to ISP A and ISP B and wants inbound traffic from the internet to prefer ISP A. Which method is commonly used?",["Prepend its own AS several times on routes sent to ISP B","Set a higher local preference on routes learned from ISP A","Set a higher weight on the ISP A neighbor","Lower the MED on routes received from ISP B"],0,"AS-path prepending makes the path through ISP B look longer to the rest of the internet, pulling inbound traffic toward ISP A. Local preference is tempting, but it only influences the enterprise's own outbound path choice and is not sent to eBGP peers.","Domain 3: BGP path selection",[null,"Local preference affects only outbound path choice inside the AS.","Weight is local to one router and affects only outbound choice.","Lowering MED on received routes affects outbound choice, not inbound traffic."],3],["x30",0,3,"A PBR route map matches ACL GUEST and sets ip next-hop 198.51.100.1. It is applied inbound on the LAN interface. What happens to packets that do not match the route map?",["They are dropped by an implicit deny in the route map","They are forwarded normally using the routing table","They are sent to the Null0 interface","They are sent to 198.51.100.1 anyway"],1,"In PBR, traffic that does not match a permit clause is not policy routed and falls back to normal destination-based routing. The implicit-deny option is tempting because ACLs work that way, but for PBR a deny means route normally, not drop.","Domain 3: Policy-based routing",["A route map deny or non-match in PBR means route normally, not drop.",null,"Traffic goes to Null0 only if a set clause says so.","Only matching traffic is sent to the set next hop."],2],["x31",0,3,"A web server at 10.1.1.20 must always be reachable from the internet at 203.0.113.20. Which NAT configuration fits?",["ip nat inside source list 10 interface Gi0/0 overload","A dynamic NAT pool containing one public address","ip nat inside source static 10.1.1.20 203.0.113.20","ip nat outside source list 20 pool OUTSIDE"],2,"Static NAT creates a permanent one-to-one mapping, so outside hosts can start connections to the server at any time. A one-address dynamic pool is tempting, but its mapping exists only after an inside host sends traffic, and it can be taken by other hosts.","Domain 3: NAT, static NAT",["This PAT config shares one address and does not allow unsolicited inbound connections.","A dynamic pool mapping appears only when the inside host sends first.",null,"Outside source NAT translates outside addresses, not the inside server."],1],["x32",0,3,"A PAT configuration looks correct, but show ip nat translations stays empty and inside hosts cannot reach the internet. What is the most likely missing piece?",["The overload keyword on the NAT statement","A default route on the ISP's router","An ACL entry permitting SSH to the router","ip nat inside and ip nat outside on the interfaces"],3,"The router only translates traffic that flows between interfaces marked inside and outside, so with no marking nothing is translated and the table stays empty. A missing overload is tempting, but without it the first host would still get a translation.","Domain 3: NAT troubleshooting",["Without overload, the first host would still get a translation entry.","The ISP's routing is not the cause of an empty local NAT table.","SSH access to the router is unrelated to NAT translation.",null],2],["x33",0,3,"Which capability does IGMPv3 add compared with IGMPv2?",["Hosts can include or exclude specific sources in reports","It removes the Leave message to cut overhead","It replaces PIM for building multicast distribution trees between the routers","It runs between routers instead of hosts and routers"],0,"IGMPv3 lets a receiver say which sources it wants (or does not want) for a group, which is what makes SSM possible. Replacing PIM is wrong because IGMP runs only between hosts and their local router; PIM still builds the trees between routers.","Domain 3: Multicast, IGMPv3",[null,"IGMPv2 introduced the Leave message, and IGMPv3 keeps leave functions.","PIM still runs between routers; IGMP is only host to router.","IGMP runs between hosts and their local router, not between routers."],1],["x34",0,4,"A port on SW1 must be monitored by an analyzer on SW3. The switches are joined by Layer 2 trunks with no routed hop between them. Which feature fits best?",["Local SPAN on SW1","RSPAN, with a remote-span VLAN carried on the trunks","ERSPAN, which any switch-to-switch mirroring requires","Flexible NetFlow export from SW1 to SW3"],1,"RSPAN carries mirrored traffic in a dedicated remote-span VLAN across Layer 2 trunks to a destination port on another switch. ERSPAN is tempting, but it is needed when the path crosses routed hops; it is not required for a pure Layer 2 path.","Domain 4: SPAN and RSPAN",["Local SPAN needs the source and destination on the same switch.",null,"ERSPAN is needed across routed hops; a Layer 2 path can use RSPAN.","NetFlow exports flow statistics, not a copy of packets."],2],["x35",0,4,"Which SNMP notification type is acknowledged by the manager and resent by the agent if no acknowledgment arrives?",["SNMPv1 trap","GetBulk response","Inform","SNMPv2c trap"],2,"An inform request expects a response from the manager, so the agent retransmits it if none arrives. The SNMPv2c trap is tempting because it shares the v2 PDU format, but traps are unacknowledged and can be silently lost.","Domain 4: SNMP traps and informs",["SNMPv1 traps are not acknowledged.","GetBulk is a manager request, not a notification from the agent.",null,"v2c traps are also unacknowledged."],1],["x36",0,4,"A router logs %LINEPROTO-5-UPDOWN: Line protocol on Interface Gi0/1, changed state to down. What does the 5 in the message represent?",["The syslog facility number","The message sequence number","The interface slot number","The severity level, notifications"],3,"In Cisco message format FACILITY-SEVERITY-MNEMONIC, the number is the severity, and 5 is notifications. The facility is tempting, but the facility is the text part (LINEPROTO) before the number.","Domain 4: Syslog severity levels",["The facility is the text before the number, here LINEPROTO.","Sequence numbers appear at the start of the line when enabled, not here.","The interface is named in the message text, not by this number.",null],1],["x37",0,4,"A ping from R1 to 10.9.9.9 succeeds, but hosts in 10.1.1.0/24 behind R1 cannot reach that address. Which test best reproduces the users' path?",["An extended ping sourced from R1's 10.1.1.1 LAN interface","The same ping from R1 with a larger repeat count","A traceroute from R1 to 10.9.9.9 using its default source address for the probes","show ip interface brief to confirm interfaces are up"],0,"Sourcing the ping from the LAN interface tests whether the far side has a return route to 10.1.1.0/24, which a default-sourced ping never checks. A default traceroute is tempting but uses the exit interface address and hides the same return-path problem.","Domain 4: Diagnosing with ping and traceroute",[null,"More repeats test the same path from the same source.","A default-sourced traceroute uses the exit interface, missing the LAN return path.","Interface status does not test the return route to the users' subnet."],2],["x38",0,4,"Which statement about RESTCONF is correct?",["It runs over SSH on port 830 using XML RPC operations","It maps HTTPS methods onto YANG data, in XML or JSON","It reads and writes SNMP MIB objects over HTTP","It can read operational data but never change configuration"],1,"RESTCONF exposes YANG-modeled data as HTTPS resources that clients read and change with methods such as GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE, encoded in XML or JSON. The SSH port 830 option describes NETCONF, the most common mix-up.","Domain 4: NETCONF and RESTCONF",["SSH port 830 and XML RPCs describe NETCONF.",null,"RESTCONF works with YANG models, not SNMP MIBs.","RESTCONF can change configuration with POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE."],2],["x39",0,4,"In Flexible NetFlow, which component defines the key and non-key fields that identify and describe a flow?",["Flow exporter","Flow monitor","Flow record","Flow sampler"],2,"The flow record lists the match (key) fields that separate flows and the collect (non-key) fields such as byte counts. The flow monitor is tempting because it ties everything together on an interface, but it references a record rather than defining the fields.","Domain 4: Flexible NetFlow",["The exporter defines where and how records are sent.","The monitor applies a record to an interface and holds the cache.",null,"A sampler controls which packets are sampled, not which fields are kept."],1],["x40",0,5,"An attacker on an access VLAN sends gratuitous ARP replies claiming the default gateway's IP address. Which switch feature checks ARP packets against the DHCP snooping binding table and drops the forged ones?",["IP Source Guard","Port security","Storm control","Dynamic ARP inspection"],3,"Dynamic ARP inspection intercepts ARP on untrusted ports and drops packets whose IP-to-MAC pairing does not match the DHCP snooping bindings. IP Source Guard is tempting because it uses the same bindings, but it filters IP traffic, not ARP messages.","Domain 5: Layer 2 protections, DAI",["IP Source Guard filters IP packets, not ARP messages.","Port security limits MAC addresses; it does not validate ARP contents.","Storm control limits traffic rates, not forged ARP.",null],1],["x41",0,5,"DHCP snooping runs on an access switch. The team must stop hosts from sending IP packets with source addresses they were not assigned. Which feature does this?",["IP Source Guard","Dynamic ARP inspection","Root guard","BPDU filter"],0,"IP Source Guard builds per-port filters from the DHCP snooping bindings and drops IP packets whose source address does not match. Dynamic ARP inspection is tempting but validates ARP only, so spoofed IP packets would still pass.","Domain 5: Layer 2 protections, IP Source Guard",[null,"DAI validates ARP; it does not filter spoofed IP packets.","Root guard protects root bridge placement, unrelated to IP spoofing.","BPDU filter stops BPDUs and has nothing to do with IP addresses."],2],["x42",0,5,"A standard ACL must stop subnet 10.1.1.0/24 from reaching the server subnet 10.3.3.0/24 while still letting it reach everything else. Where should it be applied?",["Inbound on the interface closest to 10.1.1.0/24","Outbound on the interface facing the server subnet","Inbound on the router's WAN interface","On the router's VTY lines with access-class"],1,"A standard ACL matches only the source address, so it must sit close to the destination to avoid blocking the source's traffic to other places. Placing it near the source is tempting because that is the rule for extended ACLs, but here it would cut 10.1.1.0/24 off from everything.","Domain 5: ACL placement",["Near the source, a standard ACL would block that subnet from every destination.",null,"The WAN interface is not in the path to the server subnet here.","access-class on VTY lines filters management logins, not transit traffic."],2],["x43",0,5,"An extended ACL with the single entry permit tcp any host 10.2.2.10 eq 443 is applied inbound on an interface that runs OSPF. What happens to OSPF hellos arriving on it?",["They pass, since routing protocol packets are exempt from ACLs","They pass, because this ACL filters only TCP traffic","They are dropped by the implicit deny, breaking the adjacency","They are sent to the CPU and allowed by CoPP"],2,"An inbound interface ACL also filters packets addressed to the router, and OSPF (IP protocol 89) matches nothing but the implicit deny any. The TCP-only option is tempting, but an ACL does not ignore other protocols; anything not permitted is denied.","Domain 5: ACLs, implicit deny",["Inbound interface ACLs also filter routing protocol packets to the router.","Anything not permitted, including non-TCP protocols, hits the implicit deny.",null,"The interface ACL drops the packets before CoPP would see them."],3],["x44",0,5,"A router has aaa authentication login default group radius local. The RADIUS server is reachable, but it rejects an administrator's password. What happens?",["The router tries the local user database next","The administrator is let in without authentication","The router keeps retrying the RADIUS server until it accepts the administrator's password","Login fails; local is used only if RADIUS does not respond"],3,"Method lists move to the next method only on an error such as no response; an explicit reject is a final answer. Falling back to local is the tempting choice, but that happens only when the RADIUS server is unreachable.","Domain 5: AAA method lists",["The next method is tried only on an error, not after a reject.","A rejected login is denied; the router does not skip authentication.","The server did answer; there is nothing to retry.",null],3],["x45",0,5,"Only the management subnet 10.99.0.0/24 may open SSH sessions to a router. Which configuration enforces this most directly?",["A standard ACL applied with access-class in on the VTY lines","An extended ACL applied with ip access-group out on each of the router's physical interfaces","transport input ssh on the VTY lines by itself","login local on the VTY lines with strong passwords"],0,"access-class on the VTY lines filters who may open management sessions, regardless of which interface the traffic arrives on. transport input ssh is tempting, but it only limits the protocol, not the source addresses allowed to connect.","Domain 5: Device access control",[null,"Outbound interface ACLs do not target traffic to the router's VTYs.","transport input ssh limits protocols, not source addresses.","login local sets the user database but does not restrict sources."],2],["x46",0,5,"In Cisco TrustSec, what does a security group ACL (SGACL) match on?",["Source and destination IP subnets","Source and destination security group tags","VLAN IDs on the ingress trunk","The vendor OUI in the MAC address"],1,"SGACLs are applied between groups: the policy is keyed on the source SGT and destination SGT, so it follows users regardless of IP address. IP subnets are tempting because traditional ACLs use them, but removing that dependency is the point of TrustSec.","Domain 5: TrustSec SGACL",["SGACLs remove the dependence on IP subnets.",null,"VLAN IDs are not the policy key in TrustSec.","The OUI identifies a vendor, not a security group."],1],["x47",0,5,"An IP phone and a PC share one switch port. Each must be authenticated separately with 802.1X or MAB, the phone in the voice domain and the PC in the data domain. Which host mode fits?",["Single-host mode","Multi-host mode","Multi-domain mode","Single-host mode with PortFast"],2,"Multi-domain authentication allows one authenticated device in the voice domain and one in the data domain on the same port. Multi-host is tempting, but it authenticates only the first device and then lets every other device through.","Domain 5: 802.1X host modes",["Single-host allows only one device on the port.","Multi-host authenticates one device and then lets all others in.",null,"PortFast does not change how many devices authenticate."],3],["x48",0,5,"Policy must block a file-sharing application that switches ports and hides in TLS. Which capability is best suited?",["An extended ACL denying its well-known port numbers","Stateful inspection that tracks TCP flags and sequences","A stricter CoPP policy on the edge router","NGFW application identification, independent of port"],3,"A next-generation firewall identifies applications by behavior and signatures rather than port numbers, so it can block an app that changes ports. A port-based ACL is tempting, but the app simply moves to a port the ACL allows.","Domain 5: Next-generation firewall",["A port-based ACL fails when the app switches ports.","Stateful inspection tracks connections but cannot identify applications.","CoPP protects the router's control plane, not transit traffic.",null],2],["x49",0,5,"A Python script with the Catalyst Center admin password hardcoded has been pushed to a shared Git repository. What is the best fix?",["Rotate the password and load secrets from a vault or env","Base64-encode the password inside the script","Switch the script to plain HTTP so no certificate handling is needed and the secret is simpler","Make the repository read-only for everyone"],0,"The exposed password must be treated as compromised and changed, and the script should read secrets from a vault or environment variables that never go into source control. Base64 is tempting but is encoding, not encryption, and anyone can reverse it.","Domain 5: REST API security, secret handling",[null,"Base64 is reversible encoding, not protection.","Plain HTTP would expose the credentials further in transit.","Read-only access does not remove the exposed password from history."],2],["x50",0,5,"How does TACACS+ protect its messages compared with RADIUS?",["Both encrypt the entire packet payload","TACACS+ encrypts the whole body; RADIUS hides only the password","RADIUS encrypts the whole body; TACACS+ hides only the password","Neither protocol encrypts any part of the packet"],1,"TACACS+ encrypts the entire packet body, while RADIUS obscures only the user password attribute and leaves the rest in clear text. The reversed option is the common mix-up.","Domain 5: AAA, TACACS+ vs RADIUS",["RADIUS encrypts only the password, not the whole packet.",null,"This reverses the two protocols.","TACACS+ encrypts its body, and RADIUS hides the password."],1],["x51",0,5,"A switch port is configured to try 802.1X first and then MAB. A laptop without a supplicant connects. What happens?",["The port stays unauthorized with no other method tried","The port is err-disabled because the laptop failed 802.1X, and it stays down until someone clears it","After 802.1X times out, MAB is tried with the laptop's MAC","The laptop is placed in the native VLAN automatically"],2,"With 802.1X then MAB, the switch waits for EAPOL responses, and when 802.1X times out it falls back to MAB using the device MAC. The unauthorized option is tempting, but that is what would happen only if no fallback method were configured.","Domain 5: 802.1X and MAB",["The configured MAB fallback is tried after the 802.1X timeout.","Failing 802.1X does not err-disable a port.",null,"Authorization decides the VLAN; the native VLAN is not assigned automatically."],2],["x52",0,6,"A script sends a RESTCONF POST that creates a new loopback interface, and the request succeeds. Which HTTP status code is expected?",["204 No Content","200 OK","404 Not Found","201 Created"],3,"RESTCONF returns 201 Created when a POST creates a new resource. 204 is tempting because RESTCONF uses it for successful PUT or PATCH changes with no body, but POST that creates a resource returns 201.","Domain 6: REST response codes",["204 is typical for successful PUT or PATCH, not a POST that creates a resource.","200 is used for successful GETs and some updates, not resource creation.","404 means the resource was not found, but the request succeeded.",null],3],["x53",0,6,"A script gets a token from Catalyst Center successfully but receives HTTP 403 when it tries to delete a site. What does this mean?",["The user is authenticated but not allowed to do that","The username or password sent in the delete request was wrong or expired","The site ID in the URL does not exist","The controller hit an internal server error"],0,"403 Forbidden means the server knows who you are but your role lacks permission for the action. Wrong credentials are tempting, but those return 401 Unauthorized, and the token here was issued successfully.","Domain 6: REST response codes",[null,"Bad credentials return 401, and the token was issued successfully.","A missing resource returns 404, not 403.","An internal server error returns 500."],2],["x54",0,6,"In Python, device = {'hostname': 'R1', 'ip': '10.1.1.1'}. What does device.get('os', 'unknown') return?",["It raises a KeyError","'unknown'","None","An empty string"],1,"dict.get returns the default given as its second argument when the key is missing, so the result is 'unknown'. KeyError is tempting because device['os'] would raise it, but get never raises for a missing key.","Domain 6: Python dictionaries",["get never raises KeyError; indexing with brackets would.",null,"get returns None only when no default is given.","The default is the string 'unknown', not an empty string."],1],["x55",0,6,"Which Python code sends a GET with a Catalyst Center token and parses the JSON response into Python objects?",["r = requests.get(url, headers={'X-Auth-Token': t}); d = r.text['response']","d = requests.post(url, token=t).json()","r = requests.get(url, headers={'X-Auth-Token': t}); d = r.json()","r = requests.get(url, auth=t); d = json(r)"],2,"Catalyst Center expects the token in the X-Auth-Token header, and response.json() parses the body into dicts and lists. The r.text option is tempting, but text is a plain string and cannot be indexed by a key.","Domain 6: Python requests library",["r.text is a string and cannot be indexed by a key.","The token is not a requests argument, and POST is the wrong method.",null,"auth= expects credentials, and json(r) is not a valid call."],2],["x56",0,6,"In a YANG model, what distinguishes a list from a container?",["A list can hold only one leaf node","A container has several instances, each one selected by a key leaf value","A list is always read-only operational data","A list has keyed entries; a container has one instance"],3,"A YANG list holds multiple entries, each identified by its key leaf (for example interface name), while a container simply groups nodes and appears once. The second option reverses the two, which is the usual confusion.","Domain 6: YANG data models",["A list can hold many leaves and multiple entries.","This describes a list, not a container.","Lists can hold configuration data, not just operational data.",null],2],["x57",0,6,"A team wants managed servers to run an agent that regularly pulls desired state from a central master and fixes drift on its own. Which tools match this model?",["Puppet or Chef","Ansible","NETCONF","EEM"],0,"Puppet and Chef are agent-based and pull configuration from a central server on a schedule. Ansible is tempting because it is also a configuration tool, but it is agentless and pushes changes over SSH.","Domain 6: Agent-based vs agentless orchestration",[null,"Ansible is agentless and pushes changes over SSH.","NETCONF is a device protocol, not an agent-based orchestration tool.","EEM is on-box automation for Cisco devices, not a central tool."],1],["x58",0,6,"An EEM applet must save the running configuration every day at 02:00. Which event detector should the applet use?",["event syslog pattern","event timer cron","event interface","event snmp"],1,"The timer cron event runs the applet on a schedule written as a cron entry, such as 0 2 * * *. A syslog pattern is tempting because it is the most common EEM trigger, but it fires only when a matching message is logged.","Domain 6: EEM applets",["A syslog pattern fires only when a matching message is logged.",null,"event interface reacts to interface counters, not a time of day.","event snmp watches an OID value, not a schedule."],2],["x59",0,6,"An AI-driven assurance tool flags an unusual rise in DHCP failures at one site that static thresholds did not catch. What is the most appropriate next step?",["Let the tool reboot the site's switches automatically","Ignore it, since no static threshold was crossed","Have an engineer confirm it with logs before any fix","Retrain the model before looking at the site"],2,"AI baselining is useful for spotting subtle anomalies, but a human should confirm the finding with supporting data before changing production. Automatic reboots are tempting for speed but skip the guardrail and could cause a wider outage.","Domain 6: AI in network operations",["Automatic reboots skip human review and could cause a wider outage.","Anomaly detection exists to catch problems static thresholds miss.",null,"Retraining first delays investigation of a possibly real issue."],2],["x60",0,6,"A script must turn a Python dictionary into a JSON string for a REST request body. Which function does this?",["json.loads(payload)","json.load(payload)","str(payload)","json.dumps(payload)"],3,"json.dumps serializes a Python object into a JSON-formatted string. str() is tempting, but it produces Python syntax such as single quotes and True, which is not valid JSON.","Domain 6: JSON in Python",["json.loads parses a JSON string into Python objects, the reverse.","json.load reads JSON from a file object.","str() makes Python syntax such as single quotes, which is not valid JSON.",null],1]]);
